// ConformanceCheck.maxon — the whole-program `implements`-conformance pass (P1.7a slice 1).
//
// ⭐ **IT RUNS BEFORE `resolveTypes`, and that placement IS the design.** A conformance diagnostic
// prints SOURCE type spellings — `is missing 1 method(s):\n  - increment() returns void`,
// `process(value Float) returns Integer (expected process(value Integer) returns Integer)` — and
// `resolveTypes` erases `Integer` to `int` and `Float` to `float`. So the check runs alongside
// `checkValueMergeSignatures` (`compileToCodeResult`, before the pipeline), reading the still-unresolved
// signatures; any diagnostic it records is caught by the pipeline's own error gate after `resolveTypes`.
//
// ⭐ **THE MESSAGE AND THE COMPARISON READ ONE STRING — THE SOURCE SPELLING.** An interface method carries
// its types as rendered strings (see IrInterface); a type's method carries them as `MaxonType`s in the merged
// module — rendered here through `maxonTypeName` off the same project interner. A `Self` in the interface
// substitutes the conforming type (`Movable.move returns Self` matches `Point.move returns Point`). A
// `typealias` names its own type, so `process(v Int)` does not implement `process(v Integer)` however equal
// the two ranges are, and the E3016 sentence quotes exactly what the test compared.
//
// ⭐ **DIVERGENCE FROM THE BOOTSTRAP (toward v1): an UNKNOWN interface is E3015.** The C# bootstrap
// SILENTLY accepts `implements Bogus`; v1 emits E3015, and so does shv2 — a typo'd interface name should
// not pass. See the rung report.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AN ASSOCIATED TYPE IS SUBSTITUTED ONCE, AT COLLECTION, AND EVERY LATER READER SEES ONLY THE RESULT
// (R5).** `type Box implements Container with Score` binds `Element := Score` before a single requirement is
// compared, so the requirement a verdict is computed from and the requirement a diagnostic prints ARE THE
// SAME STRING. `collectInterfaceMethods` is that one site; `signatureMatches` and
// `formatInterfaceMethodSignature` do no binding of their own. See that function's header for why this is
// v1's shape and not the bootstrap's two.

// Validate every `type X implements …` conformance whole-program (P1.7a slice 1). Records E3015 (unknown
// interface) / E3016 (partial or wrong-signature implementation); the pipeline's error gate stops the
// build. A conformance-free program short-circuits before building the function index — the same
// allocation-free gate `checkLinearAwait`/`buildEscapeSummary` use.
//
// ⚠ This used to say *"a conformance-free program (the whole scale corpus)"*, and the parenthesis was
// STALE: the generated corpus carries `y_iface_0.maxon` and `y_ifdisp.maxon`, four `implements` clauses each,
// so this check runs on every rung of the ladder (measured while attributing A1w's +3 allocations, one of
// which lands here). Do not use it as an example of the gate being taken.
// ⚠ **THE FAST PATH ASKS ABOUT ENUMS TOO SINCE enum-hashable**, because the granted-conformance walk below
// shares this function's index: a program that declares no `implements` at all may still declare an `enum`
// whose `hash`/`equals` E3130 must hold to its requirement. It costs nothing in practice — any program
// carrying the stdlib already has conformances — and skipping it would have made the new check silently
// conditional on an unrelated declaration.
export function checkConformance(project Project, module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	if project.conformances.count() == 0 and project.enumTypes.count() == 0 'nothingToCheck'
		return
	end 'nothingToCheck'

	// A duplicate-named method is E3006 (recorded by `commitFuncSignatures` during `queryAllModule`, so it
	// already sits in `project.diagnostics` here). It leaves the module's function table and the
	// `funcSignatures` registry resolving that collision independently, disagreeing on the method's arity —
	// and this check reads a method's param TYPES from the module but its param NAMES from `funcSignatures`
	// (see `checkOneMethod`), so on a disagreement it would index one column by the other's count and PANIC.
	// The E3006 already fails the build (the pipeline's error gate throws before lowering), so any E3015/E3016
	// here would be moot; refuse to read a malformed table, exactly as `PassPipeline.run` refuses to lower a
	// module that already has errors.
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'malformedModule'
		return
	end 'malformedModule'

	// The index is keyed by the REGISTRATION name (`func.name`): `qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName)`
	// for the first declaration of a method name, and the mangled `Type.method#Suffix` for every later overload
	// of it (D7) — which is exactly why `checkOneMethod` asks `project.overloadSets` for the whole set rather
	// than the bare key alone (R10).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SHARED, CHECKED NAME INDEX (`FunctionNameIndex`, A1w) — THOUGH THIS IS THE ONE OF ITS FOUR
	// SITES THAT CANNOT MEET A DUPLICATE, AND THAT IS WORTH STATING RATHER THAN INHERITING.** Two bars, either
	// sufficient: the only cause available at this point is two PARSED declarations, which is E3006 at merge
	// and which the refusal four lines above has already turned into a `return`; and every synthesizer that
	// appends to a Maxon module runs LATER (`installManagedGlobalInit` is below this call in
	// `compileToCodeResult`), so there is no synthesized side for a declaration to collide with yet. It builds
	// its index here anyway: a fourth spelling of the loop costs more than the one integer compare does, and
	// if either bar ever moves this site says so instead of silently keeping one of two functions.
	let functionsByName = try FunctionNameIndex.create(module.functions, project: project) otherwise 'duplicateFunctionName'
		return
	end 'duplicateFunctionName'
	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `project`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for declIndex in 0 upto project.conformances.count() 'eachDecl'
		checkOneConformance(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: try project.conformances.get(declIndex) otherwise panic("checkConformance: declIndex {declIndex} is below project.conformances.count()"), signatures: signatures)
	end 'eachDecl'

	checkGrantedEnumConformance(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, signatures: signatures)
	checkAssociatedTypeAbiAgreement(project, module: module, signatures: signatures)
end 'checkConformance'

// ⭐⭐ **THE GRANTED CONFORMANCES' HALF OF `checkConformance` (enum-hashable).** Everything above walks
// `project.conformances` — the `implements` clauses an AUTHOR wrote — and a payload-free `enum` writes none:
// its `Hashable`/`Equatable` is granted by the compiler (`IrInterface.isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`'s
// `payloadFreeEnum` row). That is why no requirement of it was ever matched against anything, and why a
// member colliding with one of its names went unexamined.
//
// ⛔⛔ **UNEXAMINED WAS NOT HARMLESS, BECAUSE THE MEMBER WINS.** `ProgramSignatures.enumConformanceImplName`
// stamps a declared `<Enum>.hash` into the enum's witness slot — deliberately, so that `e.hash()` and a
// `Map`/`Set` keyed by that enum reach ONE body — so a member whose shape disagrees with the requirement is
// an ABI disagreement in a dictionary slot. MEASURED on a clean compile: `hash() returns String` put a String
// record POINTER where the probe reads an i64 hash; `map.get` answered **0 against the oracle's 20** and the
// process exited **101**, a leak, with no diagnostic anywhere. It is `checkInterfaceMethods`' own argument
// for holding a `static` requirement to its shape, one grant over.
//
// ⭐ **IT ASKS `signatureMatches` — the SAME predicate a declared conformance is judged by** — so a member of
// an enum and a member of a `type X implements Hashable` cannot come to be held to two different readings of
// one requirement. What differs is only WHICH members are asked about (the ones the enum actually declares:
// a requirement the enum is silent about is served by the compiler and is not the author's to meet) and where
// the diagnostic is anchored (the METHOD's own name token — an enum has no `implements` clause to point at,
// and the member is what the author would edit).
//
// ⚠ It is called FROM `checkConformance` and takes that function's index rather than building one. The two
// walks read the same table behind the same two refusals — a module with errors, whose function table and
// `funcSignatures` can disagree about arity, and a duplicate function name — so a second
// `FunctionNameIndex.create` would be one more O(functions) map build per compile for an answer already in
// hand.
function checkGrantedEnumConformance(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	for (enumName, _) in project.enumTypes 'eachEnum'
		if not signatures.namesAPayloadFreeEnum(enumName) 'grantsNothing'
			continue
		end 'grantsNothing'

		checkOneGrantedEnumConformance(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, signatures: signatures, enumName: enumName)
	end 'eachEnum'
end 'checkGrantedEnumConformance'

// Every requirement of every protocol this enum is GRANTED, held against the member the enum declares under
// that name — if it declares one at all. The protocol list and the requirement list are both FETCHED from the
// row that grants them (`intrinsicConformanceProtocolNames` → `requireBuiltinInterface`), never transcribed,
// so the set of members this validates is the set of slots the witness table stamps, by construction.
function checkOneGrantedEnumConformance(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, signatures ProgramSignatures, enumName ByteArray)
	for protocolName in intrinsicConformanceProtocolNames(enumName, discovered: DiscoveredConformer.payloadFreeEnum) 'eachProtocol'
		let iface = requireBuiltinInterface(protocolName)
		for method in iface.methods 'eachRequirement'
			if not signatures.enumDeclaresOwnConformanceMethod(enumName, methodName: method.name) 'theCompilerServesIt'
				continue
			end 'theCompilerServesIt'

			requireGrantedEnumMemberMatches(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, signatures: signatures, enumName: enumName, protocolName: protocolName, method: method)
		end 'eachRequirement'
	end 'eachProtocol'
end 'checkOneGrantedEnumConformance'

// ONE declared member against ONE granted requirement.
//
// ⚠ **AN OVERLOADED NAME IS REFUSED RATHER THAN RESOLVED, and that is the witness slot's shape talking**: a
// slot holds exactly one address, and `enumConformanceImplName` names the BARE key, which under D7 is the
// FIRST declaration of the name — so with two members the stamped one would be decided by declaration order.
// `checkOverloadedMembers` resolves this for a declared conformance by matching the whole set and requiring
// exactly one; there is no author-written clause here to hang that on, and no corpus enum overloads either
// of these two names, so the refusal says so plainly instead of growing a second resolver.
function requireGrantedEnumMemberMatches(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, signatures ProgramSignatures, enumName ByteArray, protocolName ByteArray, method InterfaceMethodSig)
	let qualifiedName = qualifiedMethodName(enumName, methodName: method.name)

	// ⚠ A name the registry answers for but this module holds no function under cannot be validated here —
	// and it cannot be silently wrong either: the witness slot would then be stamped with a symbol nothing
	// installs, which `bakeFuncAbs64Relocs` reports outright. A LOUD failure is the safe direction, and it is
	// also the only one available: with no declaration there is no position to anchor a diagnostic on.
	if let implFunc = try functionsByName.funcNamed(qualifiedName) 'declared'
		let implSig = conformanceImplSignature(project, name: qualifiedName)
		let expected = formatInterfaceMethodSignature(method)

		if project.overloadSets.contains(qualifiedName) 'overloaded'
			reportGrantedEnumMemberMismatch(project, anchor: implFunc, enumName: enumName, protocolName: protocolName, methodName: method.name, why: "is overloaded, and a witness slot holds exactly one address", expected: expected)
			return
		end 'overloaded'

		if signatureMatches(project, method: method, conformingType: enumName, implFunc: implFunc, implSig: implSig, typeParamNames: grantedEnumTypeParamNames, signatures: signatures) 'implementsTheRequirement'
			return
		end 'implementsTheRequirement'

		reportGrantedEnumMemberMismatch(project, anchor: implFunc, enumName: enumName, protocolName: protocolName, methodName: method.name, why: "is declared {formatActualSignature(project, methodName: method.name, conformingType: enumName, implFunc: implFunc, implSig: implSig, typeParamNames: grantedEnumTypeParamNames, signatures: signatures)}", expected: expected)
	end 'declared'
end 'requireGrantedEnumMemberMatches'

// E3130's sentence, anchored on the MEMBER — the token the author would edit, and the only position an enum
// offers (its grant has no clause to blame). It says what the member IS as well as what is required, because
// the whole difficulty of this diagnostic is that the author never asked for the conformance they are being
// held to, so the sentence has to introduce it.
function reportGrantedEnumMemberMismatch(project Project, anchor IrFunction, enumName ByteArray, protocolName ByteArray, methodName ByteArray, why String, expected String)
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticGrantedConformanceMemberMismatch, message: "`enum {String.from(enumName)}` conforms to '{String.from(protocolName)}' automatically — an enum without associated values is `Hashable` and `Equatable` with no `implements` clause — and its '{String.from(methodName)}' {why}, which does not implement {expected}. That member is what a `Map`/`Set` keyed by '{String.from(enumName)}' hashes and compares through, so it must implement the requirement; rename it if it means something else", line: anchor.nameLine, column: anchor.nameColumn, filePath: anchor.sourceFilePath)
end 'reportGrantedEnumMemberMismatch'

// An `enum` declares no `uses` names, so the renderer that re-spells a member's declared types has none to
// re-spell against. Shared rather than built per member for `SignatureIndex.sharedEmptyTypeParamNames`'
// reason: it is read on a cold path, and an empty array per call is an allocation with no content.
let grantedEnumTypeParamNames = ByteArrayArray.create()

// ⭐⭐ **AN ASSOCIATED TYPE A DISPATCHED REQUIREMENT WRITES IN AN ABI POSITION HAS EXACTLY ONE LEGAL BINDING
// *WHERE NO DISPATCH SITE SAYS OTHERWISE* (E3119), AND MUST NOT BE MANAGED WHEN IT IS THE RESULT (E3120).**
// Both refusals key on the same FOUR facts, so they are decided in one walk: the interface is actually
// DISPATCHED, the associated type reaches the CALLING CONVENTION, no receiver of such a dispatch FIXED it,
// and what the conformers bound it to.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE FOURTH FACT IS W59's, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES `stdlib/Array.maxon` LISTABLE.** An associated binding
// is a property of the USE SITE (user ruling): a receiver declared `Taker with Integer` fixes `Element` for
// every dispatch on it, the emission takes the site's binding
// (`LowerMaxonToStd.associatedFormalType`, `Parser.associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance`), and
// `SemanticCheck.checkExistentialSiteClaimAtArgument` (E3127) refuses every conformer that would not keep
// that claim. So the conformers' fold is never read for such a position and nothing about their disagreement
// can reach the ABI. What is left for these two refusals is exactly the receivers that say NOTHING — a bare
// `Taker` parameter, a constrained type parameter, a field load, a merge phi — which is the shape all three
// of E3119's recorded failures have, and they stay refused.
//
// ⚠ MEASURED, the corpus case the narrowing exists for: `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s
// `from(source Iterable with (Element, Iterator with Element))` is the ONLY site in the listed stdlib that
// dispatches `Iterator`, and it names its bindings — while `ByteIterator` binds `Iterator.Element` to `Byte`
// and `WithIterIterator` binds it to a tuple. Under the old rule that pair refused every program that listed
// `stdlib/Array.maxon`, `for … in` included.
//
// ⚠ **DISPATCH IS THE GATE, AND WITHOUT IT THE CHECK FALSE-REJECTS A CORRECT PROGRAM.** Two conformers of one
// interface are only in tension if a SHARED body is compiled against both; a program whose every call is
// statically resolved has no shared body and no witness table to be wrong about. MEASURED: two conformers
// binding `Element` to `float` and to `Integer`, every call direct, ran correctly (51) and was refused.
// shv2 is whole-program, so which interfaces are dispatched is knowable exactly — `dispatchedInterfaceNames`
// reads it off the ops the parser emitted.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH ARE REFUSED ON EVERY TARGET, AND THE REGISTER TARGETS ARE WHY.** wasm traps `indirect call type
// mismatch`, which is loud; x64-windows compiled every one of these clean and answered 1 for 51, 20 for 51,
// 35 for 31, leaked (exit 101), or SEGFAULTED. A wasm-only guard would have left the silent half shipping.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A HELD POSITION TAKES THE OTHER ROUTE OUT OF THIS WALK, AND ITS GATE IS NOT THIS ONE — see
// `checkHeldAssociatedTypeBindings`.** The two obligations look alike and are triggered by different acts:
// E3119/E3120 are about a shared BODY, which only a dispatch compiles, while a holding's is about a nested
// TABLE, which a WIDENING mints. So the dispatch test moved DOWN, onto the branch it is true of — and the
// early return this function opened with, which skipped the whole walk when nothing was dispatched, had to go
// with it. What that costs is one map probe per declared interface that has `uses` names at all, on a program
// that dispatches nothing; what it bought is that `checkHeldAssociatedTypeBindings` runs at all.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE GATE IS THE DISPATCHED SLOT AND THE POSITION ITS RECEIVERS LEFT UNSETTLED, NOT THE DISPATCHED
// INTERFACE NAME, AND EACH WIDER READING WAS A FALSE REJECT.** Both refusals exist because ONE consumer
// reads the fold: `LowerMaxonToStd.witnessFormalType`
// resolves an associated FORMAL of the requirement a `witnessCall` names, and the parser types an associated
// RESULT of that same requirement. Both are asked per `(interfaceDeclIndex, methodIndex)` — never of the
// interface's other slots — so a requirement nothing dispatches has no consumer and constrains nothing. Asked
// of the interface NAME instead, `interface Iterator { current() returns Element; advance() }` was refused for
// `Element` on a program whose only dispatch is `advance()`, which cannot see `Element` at all. That is the
// same shape as the dispatch gate itself: two conformers are in tension only where a SHARED BODY is compiled
// against both, and no body is compiled for a slot no call reaches.
function checkAssociatedTypeAbiAgreement(project Project, module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	let dispatched = dispatchedRequirementSlots(module, signatures: signatures)
	for (_, iface) in signatures.interfaceMap() 'eachInterface'
		// An interface with no `uses` names has neither refusal to answer for and no holding to check, so it
		// pays neither the slot walk below nor a position loop that would iterate zero times anyway.
		if iface.associatedTypeNames.count() == 0 'noAssociatedTypes'
			continue
		end 'noAssociatedTypes'

		let written = dispatchedAssociatedNamesOf(project.interfaces, dispatched: dispatched, iface: iface)
		for position in 0 upto iface.associatedTypeNames.count() 'eachAssociatedType'
			let name = try iface.associatedTypeNames.get(position) otherwise panic("checkAssociatedTypeAbiAgreement: associatedTypeNames.get OOB at {position} below count {iface.associatedTypeNames.count()}")
			checkOneAssociatedPosition(project, signatures: signatures, written: written, iface: iface, name: name, position: position)
		end 'eachAssociatedType'
	end 'eachInterface'
end 'checkAssociatedTypeAbiAgreement'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH OBLIGATION ONE ASSOCIATED POSITION CARRIES — and they are alternatives, not a sequence.** A HELD
// position answers to `checkOneHeldPosition` and to nothing else; every other position answers to E3119/E3120
// behind the dispatch gate. One walk decides it, because the two paths need the same three facts and asking
// twice is what let the gates drift apart in the first place.
function checkOneAssociatedPosition(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, written DispatchedAssociatedNames, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, position ElementIndex)
	if let held = try signatures.associatedTypeHeldAt(iface.name, position: position) 'heldAtAnInterface'
		checkHeldAssociatedTypeBindings(project, signatures: signatures, iface: iface, name: name, position: position, heldAt: held)
		return
	end 'heldAtAnInterface'

	if not written.inAnAbiPosition.contains(name) 'noDispatchedRequirementCarriesIt'
		return
	end 'noDispatchedRequirementCarriesIt'

	checkOneAssociatedType(project, signatures: signatures, iface: iface, name: name, position: position, isDispatchedResult: written.inAResult.contains(name))
end 'checkOneAssociatedPosition'

// The two verdicts for ONE associated type of one dispatched, unheld interface position. Split out so the
// walk above reads as the gates it is. `isDispatchedResult` travels rather than being re-derived: it is the
// same slot walk the caller's gate already ran, and a second one is a second chance to disagree with it about
// which requirements this program dispatches.
function checkOneAssociatedType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, position ElementIndex, isDispatchedResult bool)
	match associatedAbiBindingOf(project.conformances, signatures: signatures, interfaceName: iface.name, position: position) 'binding'
		agreed(conformer, binding, _) then checkAgreedBindingIsCarryable(project, signatures: signatures, iface: iface, name: name, conformer: conformer, binding: binding, isDispatchedResult: isDispatchedResult)
		unconformed then break 'binding'
		disagreed(first, firstBinding, second, secondBinding) then reportAssociatedTypeBindingDisagreement(project, signatures: signatures, iface: iface, name: name, first: first, firstBinding: firstBinding, second: second, secondBinding: secondBinding)
	end 'binding'
end 'checkOneAssociatedType'

// ⭐⭐ **THE WITNESS SLOTS THIS PROGRAM DISPATCHES, PER INTERFACE — AND, PER SLOT, HOW MUCH THE LEAST
// INFORMATIVE RECEIVER SAYS (W59).** A slot index maps to the number of the dispatched interface's associated
// positions that EVERY receiver jumping through it fixed: a receiver declared `Taker with Integer` fixes one,
// a bare `Taker` parameter or a constrained type parameter fixes none, and the entry keeps the MINIMUM,
// because a position is only settled per-site if it is settled at EVERY site.
//
// It is a count and not a set of positions because a `with` clause binds a PREFIX — an instance's arguments
// are positional and `checkGenericInstance` reports a short one — so "the first k positions" is the whole of
// what a receiver can claim. A `Map` per interface rather than a flat pair key because the consumer asks it
// once per interface, walking that interface's own slot list.
typealias DispatchedSlotClaims = Map with (WitnessMethodIndex, TypeParamPosition)
typealias DispatchedSlotMap = Map with (ByteArray, DispatchedSlotClaims)

typealias AssociatedTypeNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The associated-type NAMES one interface's DISPATCHED requirements write where a witness call carries them,
// split by the two positions E3119 and E3120 gate on. `inAResult` is a subset of `inAnAbiPosition`; they are
// two sets rather than one tagged one because a name can be a parameter of one dispatched requirement and the
// result of another, and a single verdict per name could not hold both facts.
type DispatchedAssociatedNames
	export let inAnAbiPosition as AssociatedTypeNameSet
	export let inAResult as AssociatedTypeNameSet

	export static function create(inAnAbiPosition AssociatedTypeNameSet, inAResult AssociatedTypeNameSet) returns DispatchedAssociatedNames
		return Self{inAnAbiPosition: inAnAbiPosition, inAResult: inAResult}
	end 'create'
end 'DispatchedAssociatedNames'

// The answer for an interface no call dispatches — shared, never mutated, so a program whose interfaces are
// all statically resolved allocates nothing per interface for the two empty sets it would otherwise mint.
let sharedEmptyDispatchedAssociatedNames = DispatchedAssociatedNames.create(AssociatedTypeNameSet.create(), inAResult: AssociatedTypeNameSet.create())

// ⭐ **EVERY WITNESS SLOT THE PROGRAM ACTUALLY DISPATCHES THROUGH**, read off the two witness-dispatch ops the
// parser emits. Each carries the `(interfaceDeclIndex, methodIndex)` pair `LowerMaxonToStd` resolves the
// requirement through — so this asks the identical question the emission asks and cannot come to a different
// answer about which slots are live.
//
// A program that dispatches nothing (the whole corpus but the interface families) walks its ops once and
// returns an empty map, and every interface then reads the shared empty answer above.
function dispatchedRequirementSlots(module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns DispatchedSlotMap
	var slots = DispatchedSlotMap.create()
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("dispatchedRequirementSlots: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				noteDispatchedSlot(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("dispatchedRequirementSlots: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"), slots: slots, signatures: signatures, func: func)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return slots
end 'dispatchedRequirementSlots'

// Record the slot one op dispatches through, if it dispatches at all. It RANGES over the whole `MaxonOp`
// union so a newly-appended op FAILS TO COMPILE here rather than being silently swept into an ignore arm (the
// band-append invariant) — which for this walk would mean a dispatch form that quietly escapes both refusals.
function noteDispatchedSlot(op MaxonOp, slots DispatchedSlotMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, func IrFunction)
	match op 'op'
		call to switch then break 'op'
		witnessDispatch(_, args, _, methodIndex, interfaceDeclIndex, _, _) then noteOneDispatchedSlot(slots, signatures: signatures, func: func, args: args, methodIndex: methodIndex, interfaceDeclIndex: interfaceDeclIndex)
		witnessTryDispatch(_, _, args, _, methodIndex, interfaceDeclIndex, _) then noteOneDispatchedSlot(slots, signatures: signatures, func: func, args: args, methodIndex: methodIndex, interfaceDeclIndex: interfaceDeclIndex)
		panicAbortValue then break 'op'
		// The error-flag encode dispatches nothing — it is arithmetic on a value already in hand, so it fills no
		// interface slot.
		errorFlagEncode then break 'op'
		// No service op dispatches through a witness: a `spawn` calls a static factory by name, a send names
		// its message by name — both resolved at the parse — and a reply cell calls nothing.
		serviceSpawn to serviceReplyCell then break 'op'
		immortalStructRecord then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteDispatchedSlot'

// The body the two dispatch ops share — ONE interface-name resolution, feeding both the slot's key and the
// question asked of the receiver. The two arms differ only in where `args` sits in the op, so they must not
// also be two chances to key a claim to a different interface than the slot it is recorded under.
function noteOneDispatchedSlot(slots DispatchedSlotMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, func IrFunction, args ValueIdArray, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, interfaceDeclIndex InterfaceDeclIndex)
	let interfaceName = signatures.interfaceNameAtDeclIndex(interfaceDeclIndex)
	recordDispatchedSlot(slots, interfaceName: interfaceName, methodIndex: methodIndex, claimed: receiverClaimedPositions(func, signatures: signatures, args: args, interfaceName: interfaceName))
end 'noteOneDispatchedSlot'

// ⭐ **HOW MANY ASSOCIATED POSITIONS THIS DISPATCH'S RECEIVER FIXES** — the `with` arguments its declaration
// named, off the SAME `IrFunction.valueExistentialInstances` column `LowerMaxonToStd.receiverSiteBinding`
// reads to emit the formal's ABI. One column, so the refusal below and the emission cannot come to different
// answers about which receivers say what they hold.
//
// ⭐ **A CONSTRAINED TYPE PARAMETER CLAIMS WHAT ITS OWN `where` CLAUSE BOUND (G19)** — `where Source is
// Iterator with Element` is the same claim a `source Iterator with Integer` parameter makes, written on the
// constraint because a type parameter has no type position to write it in. The two answers come from two
// stores because the two receivers record the fact in two places, and both are the DECLARATION's.
//
// ZERO for every receiver that claims nothing — a bare `Taker` parameter, an UNBOUND constrained type
// parameter, a field load, a merge phi — which is the shape all three of E3119's recorded failures have, and
// is why they stay refused.
function receiverClaimedPositions(func IrFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, args ValueIdArray, interfaceName ByteArray) returns TypeParamPosition
	let receiver = witnessDispatchReceiver(args)
	if let instance = try func.valueExistentialInstances.get(receiver) 'declaredAtAnInstantiatedExistential'
		return signatures.genericInstanceArgCount(instance)
	end 'declaredAtAnInstantiatedExistential'

	let tag = try func.valueTags.get(receiver) otherwise return 0
	if tag != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notAConstrainedParameter'
		return 0
	end 'notAConstrainedParameter'

	let token = try func.valueNameIds.get(receiver) otherwise return 0
	return constrainedParamAssociatedBindings(signatures, token: token as TypeParamId, interfaceName: interfaceName).count()
end 'receiverClaimedPositions'

// ⚠ **THE INNER MAP IS REBUILT RATHER THAN WRITTEN INTO, BECAUSE A VALUE FETCHED OUT OF A `Map` BORROWS THE
// MAP** — an `upsert` on it is E3019 and an `upsert` on the outer one while that borrow is live is E3070
// (`buildDeclaredConformanceMap`'s header records the same constraint from the other side). The rebuild is
// paid once per distinct `(interface, slot)` pair AND once more each time a site claims strictly LESS than
// every site before it — bounded by the interface's associated-type count, which is a source-written arity,
// so it is still bounded by the program's requirement count and not by its op count. Every repeat that adds
// nothing — the common case, since one requirement is called from many sites and they usually claim alike —
// leaves through the probe having allocated nothing.
function recordDispatchedSlot(slots DispatchedSlotMap, interfaceName ByteArray, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, claimed TypeParamPosition)
	if slotClaimAlreadyAtOrBelow(slots, interfaceName: interfaceName, methodIndex: methodIndex, claimed: claimed) 'nothingNew'
		return
	end 'nothingNew'

	var widened = DispatchedSlotClaims.create()
	copyRecordedSlots(slots, interfaceName: interfaceName, into: widened)
	widened.upsert(methodIndex, value: claimed)
	slots.upsert(interfaceName, value: widened)
end 'recordDispatchedSlot'

// The probe half of the rebuild above, split out so the borrow of `slots` ENDS before the `upsert` — which is
// the whole reason the set is rebuilt rather than added to.
function slotClaimAlreadyAtOrBelow(slots DispatchedSlotMap, interfaceName ByteArray, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, claimed TypeParamPosition) returns bool
	if let recorded = try slots.get(interfaceName) 'thisInterfaceHasSlotsAlready'
		if let already = try recorded.get(methodIndex) 'thisSlotHasBeenSeen'
			return already <= claimed
		end 'thisSlotHasBeenSeen'
	end 'thisInterfaceHasSlotsAlready'

	return false
end 'slotClaimAlreadyAtOrBelow'

// The copy half, split for its sibling's reason. An interface with no slots yet copies nothing.
function copyRecordedSlots(slots DispatchedSlotMap, interfaceName ByteArray, into DispatchedSlotClaims)
	if let recorded = try slots.get(interfaceName) 'thisInterfaceHasSlotsAlready'
		for (slot, claimed) in recorded 'eachRecorded'
			into.upsert(slot, value: claimed)
		end 'eachRecorded'
	end 'thisInterfaceHasSlotsAlready'
end 'copyRecordedSlots'

// ⭐ **WHAT ONE INTERFACE'S DISPATCHED REQUIREMENTS DO WITH ITS ASSOCIATED TYPES — one walk of the transitive
// slot list, for both refusals.** E3119 asks whether a name reaches a witness call's calling convention at
// all and E3120 asks whether it reaches the RESULT; deriving them together is what stops the two gates from
// drifting apart on which slots this program dispatches.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE LIST IS THE TRANSITIVE ONE (`interfaceWitnessSlots`), AND `iface.methods` WAS A MEASURED ESCAPE.**
// A witness table's slots are the transitive requirement list, so `interface Derived extends Base` dispatches
// `Base`'s requirements through `Derived`'s table — and a scan of `Derived.methods` sees nothing write
// `Element`, returns early, and lets a disagreement through. MEASURED: two conformers of `Derived` binding
// `Element` to `Integer` and to `float`, dispatched through a `Derived` existential, compiled and answered
// **31 on x64 where the program computes 51**, while wasm trapped. It is also the slot numbering the op's
// `methodIndex` is against, so one list answers both halves of this walk.
//
// ⚠ **A `throws` CLAUSE IS A THIRD POSITION AN ASSOCIATED TYPE CAN REACH, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT ONE OF
// THESE.** `substituteAssociatedTypes` substitutes into it, so `function next() throws Element` is
// well-formed. It does not reach the ABI: the error travels through the dual-register flag, the error VALUE is
// an enum ordinal in a machine word whatever the clause names, and the clause is compared by NAME
// (`requireSameErrorType`) rather than lowered. MEASURED green on both targets. If a throwing witness call
// ever carries the error's own type in its signature, this walk is where that becomes a third set.
function dispatchedAssociatedNamesOf(interfaces IrInterfaceMap, dispatched DispatchedSlotMap, iface IrInterface) returns DispatchedAssociatedNames
	let slots = try dispatched.get(iface.name) otherwise 'neverDispatched'
		return sharedEmptyDispatchedAssociatedNames
	end 'neverDispatched'

	var inAnAbiPosition = AssociatedTypeNameSet.create()
	var inAResult = AssociatedTypeNameSet.create()
	let requirements = interfaceWitnessSlots(interfaces, iface: iface)
	for methodIndex in 0 upto requirements.count() 'eachRequirement'
		let claimed = try slots.get(methodIndex) otherwise 'noCallJumpsThroughThisSlot'
			continue
		end 'noCallJumpsThroughThisSlot'

		let method = (try requirements.get(methodIndex) otherwise panic("dispatchedAssociatedNamesOf: requirements.get OOB at {methodIndex} below count {requirements.count()} — the loop bounds it by that same list")).method
		if unclaimedAssociatedPosition(iface, name: method.returnTypeName, claimed: claimed) 'anUnclaimedAssociatedResult'
			inAResult.insert(method.returnTypeName)
			inAnAbiPosition.insert(method.returnTypeName)
		end 'anUnclaimedAssociatedResult'

		for paramTypeName in method.paramTypeNames 'eachFormal'
			if unclaimedAssociatedPosition(iface, name: paramTypeName, claimed: claimed) 'anUnclaimedAssociatedFormal'
				inAnAbiPosition.insert(paramTypeName)
			end 'anUnclaimedAssociatedFormal'
		end 'eachFormal'
	end 'eachRequirement'
	return DispatchedAssociatedNames.create(inAnAbiPosition, inAResult: inAResult)
end 'dispatchedAssociatedNamesOf'

// ⭐⭐ **IS `name` AN ASSOCIATED TYPE OF `iface` THAT THE DISPATCH SITES LEFT UNSETTLED? — the whole of W59's
// narrowing, in one predicate.** A name that is not associated at all answers no, exactly as `namesInclude`
// did. An associated one answers no as well once EVERY receiver through this slot fixed its position, because
// then the emission takes the SITE's binding (`LowerMaxonToStd.associatedFormalType` for the formal,
// `Parser.associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance` for the result) and the conformers' fold is never read —
// so what the conformers do or do not agree on cannot reach the ABI.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT MAKES THAT SOUND IS E3127 AND NOT THIS FUNCTION.** A site's claim decides the ABI, so only
// conformers that KEEP the claim may be widened into it; `SemanticCheck.checkExistentialSiteClaimAtArgument`
// refuses the rest, at the one place a concrete conformer and a claim meet. Narrowing here without that
// refusal in place would be E3119's own recorded failure with an extra step.
function unclaimedAssociatedPosition(iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, claimed TypeParamPosition) returns bool
	let position = try namePositionIn(iface.associatedTypeNames, name: name) otherwise return false
	return position >= claimed
end 'unclaimedAssociatedPosition'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A HOLDING OWES, AND WHY IT REPLACES E3119/E3120 RATHER THAN JOINING THEM: every conformer's
// binding must be widenable into the interface the position is held at.** E3119's rule is forced by there
// being ONE compiled body per requirement; a holding does not break that, it satisfies it a level up — the
// shared body is compiled against the EXISTENTIAL, so every conformer's result travels as the same two words
// whatever concrete cursor it bound, and the differing halves are the nested TABLES, one per conformer, in
// their own witness tables. E3120's managed-return clause lifts for the reason it was written: its stated
// cause is that the parser must type the result while the interface is only a NAME, and a holding is exactly
// that missing type — known program-wide, off the interface name, before any file parses.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND IT IS GATED ON NEITHER OF THEIR GATES, WHICH IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE.** Not on DISPATCH: the
// obligation is that `__witness_<binding>.<heldAt>` can be MINTED, and `stampAssociatedWitnessSlots` mints it
// for every table this conformer gets — which a WIDENING alone is enough to demand. MEASURED as a COMPILER
// PANIC while it was behind that gate (`witnessSlotImpl: no conformance selected a member for slot
// 'Tally.Cursor.current'`), on a program that widened a non-conforming conformer into a held existential and
// never called anything on it. Not on `requirementWritesTypeInAbiPosition` either: the slot is stamped from
// the HOLDING, so a position no requirement writes still names a nested table.
//
// Asked PER CONFORMER rather than of the fold, because a held position is precisely where the fold's one
// answer is the wrong question.
//
// The declared-conformance index is built once for the whole project and memoized, so asking it here costs
// one map probe per conformer of one interface; the walk itself is the conformance list, the same one
// `associatedAbiBindingOf` folds, and it runs only for a position some use site actually holds.
function checkHeldAssociatedTypeBindings(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, position ElementIndex, heldAt ByteArray)
	let declared = declaredConformanceIndex(project)
	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `project`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for declIndex in 0 upto project.conformances.count() 'eachDecl'
		checkOneConformersHeldBinding(project, signatures: signatures, declared: declared, iface: iface, name: name, conformance: try project.conformances.get(declIndex) otherwise panic("checkHeldAssociatedTypeBindings: declIndex {declIndex} is below project.conformances.count()"), position: position, heldAt: heldAt)
	end 'eachDecl'
end 'checkHeldAssociatedTypeBindings'

// One conformer's binding at `position`, if it declares one. ⭐ **THE CONFORMANCE ARRIVES AS A
// PARAMETER**, which is what lets it be named twice: read inline out of `project.conformances` it is a
// borrow of `project`, and `checkOneHeldBinding` writes `project`'s diagnostics, so the caller would need
// the same `try … get(declIndex) otherwise panic(…)` for the lookup AND for the conformer it passes on
// — one fact, two copies. Same cure as `Parser.conformanceRequiresMethod`.
function checkOneConformersHeldBinding(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared DeclaredConformanceMap, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, conformance ConformanceDecl, position ElementIndex, heldAt ByteArray)
	if let binding = try declAssociatedBinding(conformance, interfaceName: iface.name, position: position) 'bindsThisPosition'
		checkOneHeldBinding(project, signatures: signatures, declared: declared, iface: iface, name: name, conformer: conformance, binding: binding, heldAt: heldAt)
	end 'bindsThisPosition'
end 'checkOneConformersHeldBinding'

// One conformer's binding against the holding. TWO refusals, and the order is load-bearing: a binding that is
// ITSELF an interface fails both tests and only the first says the true thing. It is not "does not conform" —
// an existential conforms to whatever its interface does — it is that the IMPL would have to hand back two
// machine words out of a call that returns one.
//
// ⚠ **THAT CLAUSE IS E3120's, AND IT IS WORDED HERE RATHER THAN SHARED WITH `checkAgreedBindingIsCarryable`,
// BECAUSE THE OTHER ROUTE'S SENTENCE WOULD BE FALSE ON THIS ONE.** That one says the associated type "reaches
// the calling convention of a requirement this program DISPATCHES", which is the fact its gate established; a
// holding is not gated on dispatch, so on this path the sentence would assert something nothing checked.
// One code, two clauses, each true where it fires — the split E3120 already has between its own two.
function checkOneHeldBinding(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared DeclaredConformanceMap, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, conformer ConformanceDecl, binding ByteArray, heldAt ByteArray)
	if signatures.declaredNameIsFatPointer(binding) 'twoWordsOutOfAOneWordResult'
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.associatedTypeReturnNotMachineWord, message: "'{String.from(conformer.typeName)}' binds '{String.from(iface.name)}'s associated type '{String.from(name)}' to the interface type '{String.from(binding)}', and this program holds '{String.from(name)}' at the interface type '{String.from(heldAt)}' — {ExistentialIsFatPointerReason}, so the impl would have to hand BOTH words back out of a witness call that returns one. A holding supplies the second word for a CONCRETE binding, from a slot naming that conformer's own nested table; there is nothing left to supply when the binding is already an interface. Bind the associated type to a concrete conformer", line: conformer.nameLine, column: conformer.nameColumn, filePath: conformer.filePath)
		return
	end 'twoWordsOutOfAOneWordResult'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE QUESTION IS ASKED OF THE CONFORMER THE BINDING REDUCES TO, NOT OF THE RENDERED NAME.** A
	// `withArgs` entry is `renderDeclaredTypeName`'s output, so a binding to a generic instance arrives as the
	// compiler's own canonical mint (`UpCursor_Integer`) — a string the `implements` registry is not keyed by
	// and never can be, since under dictionary-passing the conformance belongs to the DECLARATION and one
	// `__witness_UpCursor.Cursor` answers for every instantiation. Asked raw, the answer was always "does not
	// implement", on `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s own `Iterable with (Element, ArrayIter)` among others.
	// `conformerNameOfDeclaredName` is the ONE reducer every other conformance door already goes through, and
	// this site was simply not going through it — the arguments are not lost by that, they are checked at the
	// instantiation SITE by `checkOneInstantiation`'s E3017, which is where a base's `where` clause lives.
	let boundConformer = signatures.conformerNameOfDeclaredName(binding).toByteArray()
	if typeConformsTo(declared, interfaces: project.interfaces, typeName: boundConformer, interfaceName: heldAt, discovered: signatures.discoveredConformerOf(boundConformer)) 'widensIntoTheHolding'
		return
	end 'widensIntoTheHolding'

	let shown = signatures.displaySpellingOfDeclaredName(binding)
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.heldAssociatedTypeBindingNotConforming, message: "'{String.from(conformer.typeName)}' binds '{String.from(iface.name)}'s associated type '{String.from(name)}' to '{String.from(shown)}', and this program holds '{String.from(name)}' at the interface type '{String.from(heldAt)}' — every value of a held associated type is widened into that existential, so every conformer's binding must conform to it, and '{String.from(shown)}' does not implement '{String.from(heldAt)}'. Declare the conformance, or hold the associated type at an interface every conformer implements", line: conformer.nameLine, column: conformer.nameColumn, filePath: conformer.filePath)
end 'checkOneHeldBinding'

// The two things a dispatch cannot carry, asked of one agreed binding. They are separate clauses because
// they fail in different POSITIONS: a fat pointer cannot be carried anywhere at all, while a managed or
// non-machine-word value is fine as a PARAMETER (the lowering resolves it and coerces the actual) and only
// breaks as a RESULT, where the front end has to type it.
function checkAgreedBindingIsCarryable(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, conformer ConformanceDecl, binding ByteArray, isDispatchedResult bool)
	if signatures.declaredNameIsFatPointer(binding) 'twoWordsThroughAOneWordSlot'
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.associatedTypeReturnNotMachineWord, message: "'{String.from(conformer.typeName)}' binds '{String.from(iface.name)}'s associated type '{String.from(name)}' to the interface type '{String.from(binding)}', and '{String.from(name)}' reaches the calling convention of a requirement this program DISPATCHES through a receiver that does not say which binding it holds — {ExistentialIsFatPointerReason}, and a witness call carries one machine word per argument and one per result — so the second word is dropped and the impl reads a witness that was never passed. Bind the associated type to a concrete type", line: conformer.nameLine, column: conformer.nameColumn, filePath: conformer.filePath)
		return
	end 'twoWordsThroughAOneWordSlot'

	checkAssociatedReturnIsUnmanagedMachineWord(project, signatures: signatures, iface: iface, name: name, conformer: conformer, binding: binding, isDispatchedResult: isDispatchedResult)
end 'checkAgreedBindingIsCarryable'

// ⭐⭐ **REFUSE AN ASSOCIATED RETURN BOUND TO ANYTHING BUT AN UNMANAGED MACHINE WORD (E3120), AND "MACHINE
// WORD" ALONE WAS THE WRONG PREDICATE.** The parser types a dispatch's result from the interface's own
// rendered spelling, which names the associated type and — at a receiver that claims nothing — can never name
// what a conformer bound it to, so the result is typed `int`. For a `float` binding that is the wrong WIDTH
// and register file. For a MANAGED binding it is the wrong OWNERSHIP: nothing takes ownership of the returned
// refcounted value and nothing releases it. MEASURED: `with String` and `with Point` both leaked, **exit 101 on x64 AND wasm**, while the
// same interface with the return SPELLED `String` instead of associated was clean — which attributes the leak
// exactly to this path.
//
// The admitted set is therefore "an unmanaged machine word", asked of the BINDING NAME because the resolved
// type has already collapsed a struct and a ranged alias to the same `integer` (`renderedTypeNameAbiType`).
//
// ⚠ **AT A RECEIVER THAT DOES SAY WHICH BINDING IT HOLDS THE PARSER *CAN* NAME IT, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS NO
// LONGER ASKED THERE (W59).** `Parser.associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance` reads the site's own `with`
// argument and types the result as that binding, ownership included, so the stated cause above is simply
// false at such a position. The gate that keeps this refusal off them is `unclaimedAssociatedPosition`, one
// walk up — the SAME gate E3119 gets, which is how these two go on keying on one set of facts.
function checkAssociatedReturnIsUnmanagedMachineWord(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, conformer ConformanceDecl, binding ByteArray, isDispatchedResult bool)
	if bindingIsUnmanagedMachineWord(signatures, binding: binding) 'travelsAsAPlainWord'
		return
	end 'travelsAsAPlainWord'

	if not isDispatchedResult 'neverADispatchedResult'
		return
	end 'neverADispatchedResult'

	// The AUTHOR's spelling: a generic-instance binding reaches here as `Box_Integer`, the canonical mint, and
	// the author wrote `IntBox`. Its two sibling refusals above print an INTERFACE name — `declaredNameIsFatPointer`
	// is true of no other declared form — so a rendered instance cannot reach them and they need no reduction.
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.associatedTypeReturnNotMachineWord, message: "'{String.from(conformer.typeName)}' binds '{String.from(iface.name)}'s associated type '{String.from(name)}' to '{String.from(signatures.displaySpellingOfDeclaredName(binding))}', and '{String.from(name)}' is the RETURN type of one of '{String.from(iface.name)}'s requirements. A dispatch's result type flows on into the code around it — which instruction the arithmetic picks, and whether the value is OWNED and released — and no dispatch through it holds a receiver that says which binding it is, so that is chosen while the interface is still only a NAME. Declare such a receiver at '{String.from(iface.name)} with <binding>', which settles the result type at the site; or bind it to an `int`, a ranged typealias or a payload-free enum; or take the value as a PARAMETER instead, where the binding IS resolved", line: conformer.nameLine, column: conformer.nameColumn, filePath: conformer.filePath)
end 'checkAssociatedReturnIsUnmanagedMachineWord'

// Can shv2 PROVE this binding is a plain, unowned machine word? The BUILTIN names it answers itself — `int`
// alone qualifies, because `float`/`bool`/`ExitCode` are not machine words at all and `String` is a managed
// byte record. Every other name is a DECLARED one, and `ProgramSignatures.declaredNameIsManaged` is the single
// home of "does a value of this declared name own a record?", the same answer a struct field's drop routing
// takes: a ranged alias and a payload-free enum are scalars there, a struct, a boxed union and a generic
// instance are not. Asking it rather than re-listing the forms is what stops this from becoming the fifth
// cascade over those registries that function exists to have collapsed.
//
// ⚠ **A NAME NO REGISTRY DECLARES IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY SAFE, AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY IT WAS.** The
// claim was that an undeclared type in a conformance is E3011 long before here. A GENERIC INSTANCE
// falsifies it: the binding is rendered as the compiler's OWN canonical name (`Box_Integer`), which no
// registry is keyed by and which no diagnostic ever fires on — measured, it reached here, answered
// "unmanaged", and the program leaked (exit 101). `declaredNameIsManaged` answers for that rendering now.
function bindingIsUnmanagedMachineWord(signatures ProgramSignatures, binding ByteArray) returns bool
	let tag = builtinTypeNameTag(binding)
	if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'aBuiltinTypeName'
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.integer
	end 'aBuiltinTypeName'

	// TWO questions, and they are genuinely different. `declaredNameIsManaged` answers OWNERSHIP — does a
	// value of this name own a record the program must release. `declaredNameIsFatPointer` answers WIDTH — an
	// existential is refcount-free and still two words, and reading that off the ownership door is what let
	// an interface-typed binding through to a SEGFAULT.
	return not signatures.declaredNameIsManaged(binding) and not signatures.declaredNameIsFatPointer(binding)
end 'bindingIsUnmanagedMachineWord'

// The E3119 message, anchored on the SECOND conformer — the declaration that introduced the disagreement,
// exactly as `checkTypeSymbolNamespace` blames the newcomer rather than the claimant that settled the name.
// Both declarations arrive ON the fold's answer, so nothing is re-derived here and nothing has to panic if a
// re-derivation misses.
//
// Both bindings are DISPLAYED rather than printed raw, for `checkAssociatedReturnIsUnmanagedMachineWord`'s
// reason: either side may be a generic instance, which a conformance records as the canonical mint.
function reportAssociatedTypeBindingDisagreement(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray, first ConformanceDecl, firstBinding ByteArray, second ConformanceDecl, secondBinding ByteArray)
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.associatedTypeAbiDisagreement, message: "'{String.from(second.typeName)}' binds '{String.from(iface.name)}'s associated type '{String.from(name)}' to '{String.from(signatures.displaySpellingOfDeclaredName(secondBinding))}', but '{String.from(first.typeName)}' binds it to '{String.from(signatures.displaySpellingOfDeclaredName(firstBinding))}' — and '{String.from(name)}' is written as a parameter or return type of a requirement this program DISPATCHES THROUGH A RECEIVER THAT DOES NOT SAY WHICH BINDING IT HOLDS. A witness dispatch is compiled ONCE for every conformer, with no per-conformer specialization, so the shared body would be compiled against one of those two types and hand the other conformer's impl bits it reads as something else. Declare that receiver at '{String.from(iface.name)} with <binding>', which settles it for that dispatch; or bind the associated type to the same type in both conformances; or give the two conformers different interfaces", line: second.nameLine, column: second.nameColumn, filePath: second.filePath)
end 'reportAssociatedTypeBindingDisagreement'

// Is `name` a PARAMETER type of ANY of `iface`'s requirements — its own AND every one projected in through
// `extends`? The one caller is `Parser.requireHeldAssociatedTypeIsOnlyReturned`, and it wants EVERY
// requirement rather than the dispatched ones `dispatchedAssociatedNamesOf` narrows to: that refusal is taken
// mid-parse, where no module of ops exists to read a dispatch off, and the holding it guards is minted by a
// WIDENING rather than by a call. Asking the transitive list is load-bearing there for the same measured
// reason it is in that walk — a parameter projected in from a parent is one `iface.methods` cannot see.
export function requirementTakesType(interfaces IrInterfaceMap, iface IrInterface, name ByteArray) returns bool
	for slot in interfaceWitnessSlots(interfaces, iface: iface) 'eachRequirement'
		for paramTypeName in slot.method.paramTypeNames 'eachFormal'
			if paramTypeName.equals(name) 'inAnArgument'
				return true
			end 'inAnArgument'
		end 'eachFormal'
	end 'eachRequirement'
	return false
end 'requirementTakesType'

// The interfaces a type is DECLARED to implement — `Point` -> {`Digest`}. Built from `project.conformances`
// for the E3017 where-constraint check: an instantiation `Box with Point where T is Digest` is legal iff
// `Point` declares `implements Digest`. Conformance is DECLARED (nominal), matching `checkConformance`'s model.
export typealias DeclaredConformanceMap = Map with (ByteArray, InterfaceNameSet)

// ⭐ **WHERE-CONSTRAINT VALIDATION AT INSTANTIATION (P1.7a slice 2a — E3017).** For every `typealias X = Base
// with Args` whose base declares `where T is <Interface>` constraints, each concrete argument must conform to
// (declare `implements`) the interface its parameter is constrained to — else E3017, anchored on the
// instantiation. Runs AFTER `queryProgramSignatures` (the instances are interned by then) and reads the
// DECLARED conformances `checkConformance` already validated (E3016), so a type reaching here as conforming has
// a complete, correct implementation. A program with no constrained instantiation short-circuits allocation-free.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AN INSTANTIATION NEED NOT HAVE BEEN WRITTEN AS A `typealias`, AND THE ONE THAT IS NOT WAS THE ONE
// GOING UNCHECKED (W41-lit).** `[k: v]` interns a `Map with (K, V)` from its first pair — a real
// instantiation of a real constrained generic, with a real source position and no type name anywhere in the
// program. The token SWEEP that fills `instantiationSites` reads `typealias` declarations, so it structurally
// cannot see one; the PARSER records it instead (`Project.inferredInstantiationSites`). Both lists are walked
// here, by one loop over one rule, because *"does this type argument satisfy the base's `where` clause"* is
// one question and a second copy of it would be free to answer the literal differently from the alias.
export function checkWhereConstraints(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	let aliasSites = signatures.instantiationSites()
	// ⭐ **A THIRD PROVENANCE: a literal written at FILE SCOPE.** `var m = [b"if": Info.create(…)]` interns a
	// `Map with (K, V)` from its first pair exactly as a body's literal does, but the constant evaluator is a
	// throwaway `Parser` whose artifact is never committed — so its sites are recorded on the index instead
	// (`ProgramSignatures.constInferredInstantiationSites`). Without this list a file-scope literal keyed on a
	// type implementing NEITHER of `Map`'s constraints was admitted in silence, which is the identical hole
	// `project.inferredInstantiationSites` was added to close one position over.
	let constSites = signatures.constInferredInstantiationSites()
	if aliasSites.count() == 0 and project.inferredInstantiationSites.count() == 0 and constSites.count() == 0 'noSites'
		return
	end 'noSites'

	// A prior diagnostic (a parse error, an E3006 duplicate) leaves `project.conformances` possibly INCOMPLETE
	// — a file whose parse failed may not have merged its `implements` clauses — so a conforming type could
	// read as non-conforming and yield a SPURIOUS E3017. Refuse to check a malformed program, exactly as
	// `checkConformance` does: the real diagnostic already fails the build, and this one would only be noise.
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'malformedProject'
		return
	end 'malformedProject'

	let declared = declaredConformanceIndex(project)
	// The DECLARED sites first, so a program whose alias and whose literal both name an inadmissible argument
	// is reported against the declaration the author wrote before the one the compiler inferred.
	checkInstantiationSites(project, signatures: signatures, declared: declared, sites: aliasSites)
	checkInstantiationSites(project, signatures: signatures, declared: declared, sites: project.inferredInstantiationSites)
	checkInstantiationSites(project, signatures: signatures, declared: declared, sites: constSites)
end 'checkWhereConstraints'

// Validate every site in one list. The two lists this check reads differ in where they were RECORDED — a
// `typealias` by the token sweep, a bracketed literal by the parser — and in nothing the rule can see, so
// the walk is written once rather than once per provenance.
function checkInstantiationSites(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared DeclaredConformanceMap, sites GenericInstantiationSiteArray)
	for site in sites 'eachSite'
		checkOneInstantiation(project, signatures: signatures, declared: declared, site: site)
	end 'eachSite'
end 'checkInstantiationSites'

// ⭐ **`<Type> from "…"` REQUIRES `implements InitableFromStringLiteral` — checked HERE, at the merge, for
// `checkWhereConstraints`' reason (BATCH2 slice 3).** The construction is sugar for `<Type>.init(<literal>)`
// and the parser emits exactly that call; what it CANNOT decide at the construction site is whether the type
// declares the conformance, because the clause is recorded when the type's own file is parsed and the two
// files have no ordering. So the parser records the SITE (see `LiteralInitSite`) and the verdict is taken
// here, off the one conformance store `checkConformance` validated.
//
// The diagnostic is the ORACLE'S, word for word — `E3005: Type 'Wrapper' does not conform to
// InitableFromStringLiteral`, anchored on the type-name token (measured against the bootstrap on the same
// program) — so a `/specs` case pinning it is portable between the two compilers with no edit.
//
// ⚠ **A NON-CONFORMING TYPE IS THE ONLY THING LEFT TO CHECK, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS THE WHOLE PASS.** An
// UNDECLARED name never reaches here (the parser's arm requires a declared struct, so `Bogus from "x"` stays
// `E2004 Undefined variable`, which is also what the oracle answers); a missing or wrongly-typed `init` is
// the ordinary unknown-callee / argument check against `<Type>.init`, raised where every other call raises it.
// A program with no such construction — every one in the suite but two specs — short-circuits allocation-free.
export function checkLiteralInitConformance(project Project)
	if project.literalInitSites.count() == 0 'noConstructions'
		return
	end 'noConstructions'

	// A prior diagnostic may leave `project.conformances` INCOMPLETE — a file whose parse failed has not
	// merged its `implements` clauses — so a conforming type could read as non-conforming and earn a
	// SPURIOUS E3005. Refuse to check a malformed program, exactly as `checkWhereConstraints` does.
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'malformedProject'
		return
	end 'malformedProject'

	let declared = declaredConformanceIndex(project)
	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `project`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for siteIndex in 0 upto project.literalInitSites.count() 'eachSite'
		let site = try project.literalInitSites.get(siteIndex) otherwise panic("checkLiteralInitConformance: siteIndex {siteIndex} is below project.literalInitSites.count()")
		if not typeDeclaresInterface(declared, interfaces: project.interfaces, typeName: site.typeName, interfaceName: site.interfaceName) 'doesNotConform'
			// The POSITION is read off the borrow and copied into scalars, so the only thing crossing the
			// mutating call is a fresh inline element read — see the loop header's note.
			let nameLine = site.nameLine
			let nameColumn = site.nameColumn
			// The conformance the site requires, taken off the borrow into an OWNED String for the reason the
			// two positions above are: what crosses the mutating call must not be a read of `project`.
			let interfaceName = String.from(site.interfaceName)
			reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.typeMismatch, message: "Type '{String.from((try project.literalInitSites.get(siteIndex) otherwise panic("checkLiteralInitConformance: siteIndex {siteIndex} is below project.literalInitSites.count()")).typeName)}' does not conform to {interfaceName}", line: nameLine, column: nameColumn, filePath: (try project.literalInitSites.get(siteIndex) otherwise panic("checkLiteralInitConformance: siteIndex {siteIndex} is below project.literalInitSites.count()")).filePath)
		end 'doesNotConform'
	end 'eachSite'
end 'checkLiteralInitConformance'

// Validate one instantiation site's every `where` constraint (P1.7a slice 2a). A base with no constraints
// (the whole non-generic-witness corpus) iterates zero times.
function checkOneInstantiation(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared DeclaredConformanceMap, site GenericInstantiationSite)
	let baseName = signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(site.gid)
	// ⭐⭐ **AN `interface` BASE IS AN EXISTENTIAL, NOT A CONTAINER, AND ITS ARGUMENTS ARE A DIFFERENT
	// OBLIGATION.** `Seq with Integer` binds an associated type; `Box with Integer` fills a type parameter with
	// a `where` clause to satisfy. Both are `Base with Args` and both are recorded as the same instantiation
	// site, so the split is made here, at the one walk over those sites, rather than by a second list.
	match lookupInterface(project.interfaces, name: baseName) 'base'
		found(iface) then checkInterfaceInstantiationBindings(project, signatures: signatures, site: site, iface: iface)
		notFound then checkStructInstantiationConstraints(project, signatures: signatures, declared: declared, site: site, baseName: baseName)
	end 'base'
end 'checkOneInstantiation'

// ⭐⭐ **A WRITTEN `with` ARGUMENT ON AN EXISTENTIAL IS A CLAIM, AND E3125 IS WHAT MAKES IT ONE.**
// `ProgramSignatures.instanceDenotedType` does not carry the arguments onto the type — the conformances
// already record them once, and E3119 is what makes that record UNIQUE for any interface the program
// dispatches through. So a use site restates a settled fact, and a restatement that contradicts it must be
// refused: unchecked it would have no effect whatever, while reading as though it chose the binding.
//
// ⚠ **THE COMPARISON IS RAW, EXACTLY AS `associatedAbiBindingOf`'s IS BETWEEN TWO CONFORMERS.** Collapsing a
// ranged alias to its primitive first would make `Integer` and a `Score = int(0 to 100)` one binding here
// while E3119 holds them apart one function away — and E3119 holds them apart for a measured reason: the
// actual 5000 answered 5000 through one and RANGE-PANICKED through the other.
function checkInterfaceInstantiationBindings(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, site GenericInstantiationSite, iface IrInterface)
	let bound = signatures.genericInstanceArgCount(site.gid)
	for position in 0 upto iface.associatedTypeNames.count() 'eachAssociatedType'
		// An instantiation that binds FEWER arguments than the interface declares is `checkGenericInstance`'s
		// arity report; it claims nothing at this position and is passed over rather than read out of bounds.
		if position >= bound 'claimsNothingHere'
			continue
		end 'claimsNothingHere'

		checkOneAssociatedBinding(project, signatures: signatures, site: site, iface: iface, position: position)
	end 'eachAssociatedType'
end 'checkInterfaceInstantiationBindings'

// One position's verdict, split out so the walk above reads as the arity gate it is. The two arms that are
// NOT the claim check are silent on purpose: `unconformed` means no value can ever be widened into this
// existential, so nothing constrains the spelling; `disagreed` is E3119's, reported against both conformers
// with their own positions, and a second sentence here would blame the use site for the conformers' quarrel.
function checkOneAssociatedBinding(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, site GenericInstantiationSite, iface IrInterface, position ElementIndex)
	// ⭐⭐ **A HELD POSITION'S ARGUMENT IS NOT A RESTATEMENT OF A CONFORMER'S BINDING, SO IT IS NOT COMPARED
	// WITH ONE.** It NAMES the holding — `Bag with (Integer, IntegerCursor)` where the conformers bind
	// `UpCursor` and `DownCursor` is exactly right, and the raw comparison below would report the
	// single-conformer spelling of it (`agreed`, `UpCursor` ≠ `IntegerCursor`) as a contradiction. What it
	// must agree with is the holding the program SETTLED, which is the check one function down; the
	// conformers' side of the same claim is `checkHeldAssociatedTypeBindings`, against every one of them
	// rather than against the fold's single answer.
	if let held = try signatures.associatedTypeHeldAt(iface.name, position: position) 'heldAtAnInterface'
		requireWrittenBindingNamesTheHolding(project, signatures: signatures, site: site, iface: iface, position: position, heldAt: held)
		return
	end 'heldAtAnInterface'

	// ⭐⭐ **AN OPAQUE ARGUMENT IS A CLAIM ABOUT EACH INSTANTIATION, NOT ABOUT THE PROGRAM, SO IT IS DEFERRED TO
	// THE WIDENING (W58, E3127).** The comparison below rests on the argument naming a TYPE, and the enclosing
	// generic's own parameter does not: `typealias TakerOfT = Taker with T` inside `type Box uses T` says
	// nothing that could agree or disagree with `Runner`'s `Integer` — it says something different at every
	// `Box with X`, and `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `Iterable with (Element, ElementIterator)` is the shape of it.
	// Compared raw it read as a contradiction and refused a program that computes.
	//
	// ⚠ **DEFERRED, NOT DROPPED, AND THE DISCHARGE IS WHAT MAKES THE DEFERRAL SOUND.** The claim is paid at the
	// WIDENING (`SemanticCheck.checkExistentialSiteClaimAtArgument`), where the call's own instance has fixed
	// the parameter and a concrete conformer is actually crossing into the existential — and an argument still
	// opaque THERE is refused rather than admitted, which is what keeps the `return` position closed.
	//
	// ⚠ It sits BELOW the held-position arm on purpose. A holding is not a restatement of a conformer's binding
	// at all (see that arm), and `T` names no interface to hold a position at, so deferring it there would drop
	// a real refusal rather than move one.
	if maxonTypeTag(signatures.substitutedInstanceArg(site.gid, position: position as TypeParamPosition)) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'deferredToTheWidening'
		return
	end 'deferredToTheWidening'

	match associatedAbiBindingOf(project.conformances, signatures: signatures, interfaceName: iface.name, position: position) 'binding'
		unconformed then break 'binding'
		disagreed then break 'binding'
		agreed(conformer, binding, _) then requireWrittenBindingMatchesConformers(project, signatures: signatures, site: site, iface: iface, position: position, conformer: conformer, binding: binding)
	end 'binding'
end 'checkOneAssociatedBinding'

// ⭐ **A SECOND USE SITE MAY NOT HOLD ONE POSITION AT A DIFFERENT INTERFACE — nor at a concrete type once
// another site has held it.** The holding is that position's ABI binding, and it is unique for E3119's exact
// reason one level up: the requirement's shared body is compiled once, against the interface the position is
// held at, and every dispatch through it reads the nested table out of a slot laid out for that one holding.
// The settle records the FIRST site's answer, so a contradicting one is refused here rather than silently
// losing — which is what "a use site restates a settled fact" means when the fact is a holding.
function requireWrittenBindingNamesTheHolding(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, site GenericInstantiationSite, iface IrInterface, position ElementIndex, heldAt ByteArray)
	if let written = try signatures.instanceArgHeldInterfaceName(site.gid, position: position as TypeParamPosition) 'namesAnInterface'
		if written.equals(heldAt) 'namesTheSettledHolding'
			return
		end 'namesTheSettledHolding'
	end 'namesAnInterface'

	let name = try iface.associatedTypeNames.get(position) otherwise panic("requireWrittenBindingNamesTheHolding: associatedTypeNames.get OOB at {position} below count {iface.associatedTypeNames.count()} — the caller's loop bounds this position by that same list")
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.interfaceInstantiationBindingDisagreement, message: "this use of '{String.from(iface.name)}' binds its associated type '{String.from(name)}' to '{String.from(signatures.instanceArgDisplayName(site.gid, position: position as TypeParamPosition))}', but this program HOLDS '{String.from(name)}' at the interface type '{String.from(heldAt)}' — a held position has exactly one holding, because the requirement's shared body is compiled once against it and every dispatch reads the nested table from a slot laid out for that one interface. Write '{String.from(heldAt)}', or give the two readings different interfaces", line: site.nameLine, column: site.nameColumn, filePath: site.filePath)
end 'requireWrittenBindingNamesTheHolding'

function requireWrittenBindingMatchesConformers(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, site GenericInstantiationSite, iface IrInterface, position ElementIndex, conformer ConformanceDecl, binding ByteArray)
	// The SOURCE spelling, which is the side a conformance's `withArgs` is also recorded in — comparing the
	// compiled name against a source string would report a difference in RENDERING as a difference in type.
	let written = signatures.instanceArgDisplayName(site.gid, position: position as TypeParamPosition)
	if written.equals(binding) 'restatesTheSettledBinding'
		return
	end 'restatesTheSettledBinding'

	let name = try iface.associatedTypeNames.get(position) otherwise panic("requireWrittenBindingMatchesConformers: associatedTypeNames.get OOB at {position} below count {iface.associatedTypeNames.count()} — the caller's loop bounds this position by that same list")
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.interfaceInstantiationBindingDisagreement, message: "this use of '{String.from(iface.name)}' binds its associated type '{String.from(name)}' to '{String.from(written)}', but '{String.from(conformer.typeName)}' binds it to '{String.from(binding)}' — an interface this program DISPATCHES through has exactly one binding per associated type (E3119's rule), so a use site does not choose one, it states the one the conformances already settled. Write the binding the conformers declare, or give the two readings different interfaces", line: site.nameLine, column: site.nameColumn, filePath: site.filePath)
end 'requireWrittenBindingMatchesConformers'

// Validate one STRUCT instantiation's every `where` constraint (P1.7a slice 2a). A base with no constraints
// (the whole non-generic-witness corpus) iterates zero times.
function checkStructInstantiationConstraints(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared DeclaredConformanceMap, site GenericInstantiationSite, baseName ByteArray)
	let constraints = signatures.structWhereConstraints(baseName)
	for i in 0 upto constraints.count() 'eachConstraint'
		let constraint = try constraints.get(i) otherwise panic("checkStructInstantiationConstraints: whereConstraints.get OOB at {i}")
		if instantiationMeetsConstraint(signatures, interfaces: project.interfaces, declared: declared, gid: site.gid, constraint: constraint) 'satisfied'
			checkConstraintAssociatedBindings(project, signatures: signatures, site: site, constraint: constraint, baseName: baseName)
			continue
		end 'satisfied'

		reportUnsatisfiedConstraint(project, signatures: signatures, site: site, constraint: constraint, baseName: baseName)
	end 'eachConstraint'
end 'checkStructInstantiationConstraints'

// ⭐⭐ **A `where` CONSTRAINT'S BINDING IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE TYPE ARGUMENT, AND THIS IS WHERE IT IS CHECKED
// (E3131, G19).** `where Source is Iterator with Element` states that the conformance behind `Source` binds
// `Iterator`'s `Element`, and `Parser.associatedReturnThroughConstraintBinding` compiles the shared body
// against that answer. So only a conformer that KEEPS the claim may be supplied — the identical obligation
// E3127 imposes on a value widened into a parameterized existential, one door over, and for the identical
// reason: a claim decides the ABI, so admitting a conformer that binds otherwise reads its bits as the wrong
// type.
//
// It runs BESIDE the E3017 verdict rather than inside it, and only when that verdict passed: a type argument
// that does not conform at all has no binding to compare, and one refusal per instantiation is what an author
// can act on.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT COMPARES RENDERED NAMES, SO IT MAY ONLY RUN WHERE BOTH RENDERINGS ARE CONCRETE — AND THE FIRST
// CUT DID NOT, WHICH REFUSED `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon` OUTRIGHT.** A rendering written over type PARAMETERS
// is a name in ONE declaration's scope, not a type identity: `extension Iterable`'s
// `WithIterIterator with (Iter, Element)` claims `Iterator with <Iterable's Element>` while `MapIterator`'s
// own conformance binds `Iterator`'s `Element` to `<MapIterator's (Key, Value)>` — the SAME tuple, spelled
// `__Tuple2.Tfcd8090be60770aa.T5d84c0fc6312cff6` on one side and `__Tuple2.T38ee35d378b734f4.Taebb70a7666c1b3c`
// on the other, because the parameter TOKENS belong to different declarations. MEASURED: E3131 on the
// corpus's own `withIterator`, on a claim that is true.
//
// ⇒ two gates, and between them they are what makes a MISMATCH mean a wrong program:
//   • the INSTANTIATION must be fully concrete (`instanceArgsAreFullyConcrete`) — a parametric one is a
//     shape inside some shared body, compiled against that declaration's own view, and it says nothing a
//     name comparison can decide;
//   • the SUPPLIED binding must reduce to a concrete rendering (`suppliedConstraintBinding`), which for a
//     GENERIC conformer means substituting its own `withArgs` through this argument's instantiation.
// An inconclusive comparison is SKIPPED rather than reported: this refuses a wrong program, and a rendering
// it cannot make concrete is not evidence of one.
function checkConstraintAssociatedBindings(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, site GenericInstantiationSite, constraint WhereConstraint, baseName ByteArray)
	if not signatures.instanceArgsAreFullyConcrete(site.gid) 'aParametricInstantiation'
		return
	end 'aParametricInstantiation'

	for position in 0 upto constraint.associatedBindings.count() 'eachBoundPosition'
		let claimed = substitutedConstraintBinding(signatures, gid: site.gid, baseName: baseName, binding: try constraint.associatedBindings.get(position) otherwise panic("checkConstraintAssociatedBindings: associatedBindings.get OOB at {position} below count {constraint.associatedBindings.count()}"))
		let supplied = try suppliedConstraintBinding(signatures, conformances: project.conformances, gid: site.gid, constraint: constraint, position: position) otherwise continue
		if claimed.equals(supplied) 'theArgumentKeepsTheClaim'
			continue
		end 'theArgumentKeepsTheClaim'

		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticWhereConstraintBindingDisagreement, message: "'{String.from(baseName)}' constrains type parameter '{String.from(constrainedTypeParamName(signatures, baseName: baseName, position: constraint.typeParamIndex as FieldIndex))}' to '{String.from(constraint.interfaceName)} with {String.from(claimed)}', but '{String.from(signatures.instanceArgDisplayName(site.gid, position: constraint.typeParamIndex))}' binds '{String.from(constraint.interfaceName)}'s associated type '{String.from(associatedTypeNameAt(project.interfaces, interfaceName: constraint.interfaceName, position: position))}' to '{String.from(supplied)}' — a `where` constraint's binding is what types every dispatch through that parameter inside the shared body, which is compiled ONCE, so a conformer binding it otherwise would have its bits read as the claimed type. Bind the constraint to what the conformer declares, or supply an argument whose conformance binds what the constraint states", line: site.nameLine, column: site.nameColumn, filePath: site.filePath)
		return
	end 'eachBoundPosition'
end 'checkConstraintAssociatedBindings'

// The name at one associated POSITION, for a diagnostic that has the position and owes the reader the word
// the author wrote. Reaching here means a conformance for that interface was already read at that position,
// so a missing declaration or a short `uses` list is an internal disagreement rather than a user error.
function associatedTypeNameAt(interfaces IrInterfaceMap, interfaceName ByteArray, position ElementIndex) returns ByteArray
	match lookupInterface(interfaces, name: interfaceName) 'resolve'
		notFound then panic("associatedTypeNameAt: interface '{String.from(interfaceName)}' resolves to nothing, and a constraint naming it was just accepted against a conformance")
		found(iface) then return try iface.associatedTypeNames.get(position) otherwise panic("associatedTypeNameAt: '{String.from(interfaceName)}' declares {iface.associatedTypeNames.count()} associated type(s) and position {position} was bound against it — the `with` clause's length is checked against that same list")
	end 'resolve'
end 'associatedTypeNameAt'

// A constraint's binding is written in the DECLARING type's own scope, so a binding that names one of its
// `uses` parameters means whatever THIS instantiation supplied there — `where S is Cursor with E` at
// `Wrap with (IntCur, Integer)` claims `Integer`. Every other binding is a concrete name already and travels
// unchanged, which is why an unsubstitutable one falls back to itself here while the SUPPLIED side treats the
// same miss as inconclusive: a claim the author wrote is a name in the program, a conformer's `withArgs`
// entry over its own parameters is not.
function substitutedConstraintBinding(signatures ProgramSignatures, gid GenericInstanceId, baseName ByteArray, binding ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return try substitutedBindingIn(signatures, gid: gid, paramNames: signatures.structTypeParamsOf(baseName), binding: binding) otherwise binding
end 'substitutedConstraintBinding'

// ⭐ **THE ONE SUBSTITUTION STEP — a rendered name written over `paramNames`, rewritten into the scope
// `gid` instantiates them in.** `MapError.keyNotFound` where the binding is not a bare parameter name (a
// tuple or a nested instance is a rendering this cannot rewrite) or where the instantiation bound nothing at
// that position. The answer is the SOURCE spelling (`instanceArgDisplayName`), which is the side a
// conformance's `withArgs` is recorded in — comparing a compiled name against a source string would report a
// difference in RENDERING as a difference in type, exactly as `requireWrittenBindingMatchesConformers` records.
function substitutedBindingIn(signatures ProgramSignatures, gid GenericInstanceId, paramNames ByteArrayArray, binding ByteArray) returns ByteArray throws MapError
	let position = try namePositionIn(paramNames, name: binding)
	if position >= signatures.genericInstanceArgCount(gid) 'boundOutsideThisInstantiation'
		throw MapError.keyNotFound
	end 'boundOutsideThisInstantiation'

	return signatures.instanceArgDisplayName(gid, position: position)
end 'substitutedBindingIn'

// What the ARGUMENT at this constrained position actually binds, as a CONCRETE rendering — or
// `MapError.keyNotFound` where no concrete one can be derived, which the caller reads as *"say nothing"*.
// A miss is also what an under-bound conformance produces, and that is E3016's to report rather than this
// code's to guess at.
//
// ⭐ **A GENERIC CONFORMER WRITES ITS BINDING OVER ITS OWN PARAMETERS, so the name it declares becomes a type
// only once THIS argument's instantiation is substituted into it** — `MapIterator implements Iterator with
// (Key, Value)` states a tuple of two parameters, and `MapIterator with (String, Integer)` is what turns it
// into one. A bare parameter name substitutes to that instantiation's own display name; any richer rendering
// (a tuple, a nested instance) is one this comparison cannot make concrete, and it says nothing.
function suppliedConstraintBinding(signatures ProgramSignatures, conformances ConformanceDeclArray, gid GenericInstanceId, constraint WhereConstraint, position ElementIndex) returns ByteArray throws MapError
	let conformer = signatures.conformerTypeArgName(gid, position: constraint.typeParamIndex).toByteArray()
	let binding = try conformerAssociatedBinding(conformances, typeName: conformer, interfaceName: constraint.interfaceName, position: position)
	let conformerParams = signatures.structTypeParamsOf(conformer)
	if conformerParams.isEmpty() 'aNonGenericConformerBindsAConcreteType'
		return binding
	end 'aNonGenericConformerBindsAConcreteType'

	let typeArg = signatures.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: constraint.typeParamIndex)
	if maxonTypeTag(typeArg) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'noInstantiationToSubstituteThrough'
		throw MapError.keyNotFound
	end 'noInstantiationToSubstituteThrough'

	return try substitutedBindingIn(signatures, gid: genericInstanceIdOf(typeArg), paramNames: conformerParams, binding: binding)
end 'suppliedConstraintBinding'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES ONE `where` CONSTRAINT HOLD AT ONE INSTANTIATION? — THE VERDICT, ONCE, FOR THE TWO READERS THAT
// DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT TO DO WITH IT.** `checkStructInstantiationConstraints` above REPORTS an unmet one
// (E3017); `ProgramSignatures.instantiationViolatesItsOwnWhereClause` SUPPRESSES a downstream refusal that
// would otherwise speak over it (W90). One rule, because a suppression that disagreed with the report about
// which instantiations are unsatisfiable would either silence a live refusal or let the masked one back in.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE CONFORMANCE INDEX IS THE CALLER'S, and a SUPPRESSION MAY ONLY EVER PASS A SUPERSET OF WHAT THE
// REPORT READS.** The report reads the merged `project.conformances` (and refuses to run at all on a
// malformed project — see `checkWhereConstraints`); the suppression reads what a PURE parser can reach,
// which is `ProgramSignatures.sweptConformanceIndex` ORed with
// `ProgramSignatures.extensionDeclaredConformances`. The asymmetry is the whole rule and it is NOT
// symmetric: a false `satisfied` at the suppression merely leaves the pre-W90 over-refusal standing, which
// is loud; a false `unmet` withholds a refusal the report then does NOT raise, which is a program that
// should be refused compiling in silence. The swept index ALONE is strictly weaker than the merged one — it
// never holds an `extension <T> implements <I>` — and this header once argued the opposite direction was the
// safe one. See `ProgramSignatures.instantiationViolatesItsOwnWhereClause` for the access violation that
// measured it.
//
// The CANONICAL conformer name (`Integer` -> `int`) is what the conformance test compares by — the same
// name the witness table is keyed by — while the E3017 MESSAGE still prints the source spelling.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AN OPAQUE TYPE ARGUMENT IS DISCHARGED BY ITS OWNER'S OWN `where` CLAUSE, NOT BY A CONFORMER
// NAME (W41).** A generic body instantiating another constrained generic binds that callee's
// constrained parameter to its OWN type parameter — `Map with (Key, Value)`'s body building a
// `MapIterator with (Key, Value)`. `Key` is opaque, so it reduces to a MANGLED token no `implements`
// claims, and the conformer test below refuses it: under dictionary-passing that refuses EVERY generic
// that drives another generic. The oracle accepts exactly these programs — measured on a stdlib-free
// 45-line reproducer where the bootstrap compiles and prints `thing` and shv2 answered
// `E3017: Type 'T4032a9ecb1272bfc' does not satisfy constraint 'Named'`.
//
// The verdict is SHARED with the lowering that must then supply the witness pointer; see
// `opaqueParamDischarge` for why acceptance and slot index are one answer and not two.
export function instantiationMeetsConstraint(signatures ProgramSignatures, interfaces IrInterfaceMap, declared DeclaredConformanceMap, gid GenericInstanceId, constraint WhereConstraint) returns bool
	let typeArg = signatures.substitutedInstanceArg(gid, position: constraint.typeParamIndex)
	if maxonTypeTag(typeArg) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaqueArgument'
		return opaqueConstraintIsDischarged(opaqueParamDischarge(signatures, interfaces: interfaces, token: typeParamIdOf(typeArg), interfaceName: constraint.interfaceName))
	end 'opaqueArgument'

	let conformerType = signatures.conformerTypeArgName(gid, position: constraint.typeParamIndex).toByteArray()
	return typeConformsTo(declared, interfaces: interfaces, typeName: conformerType, interfaceName: constraint.interfaceName, discovered: signatures.discoveredConformerOf(conformerType))
end 'instantiationMeetsConstraint'

// The ONE E3017 sentence, for the two arms that reach it — a CONCRETE argument whose type declares no
// conformance, and an OPAQUE one whose owner declaration promises nothing (W41). Split out when the second
// arm landed: one diagnostic written twice is one that comes to disagree with itself about which name it
// blames, and the SOURCE-spelling half (`instanceArgDisplayName`, which keeps `IntWrapper` where the
// comparison uses the canonical name) is exactly the half that would drift.
function reportUnsatisfiedConstraint(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, site GenericInstantiationSite, constraint WhereConstraint, baseName ByteArray)
	let sourceType = signatures.instanceArgDisplayName(site.gid, position: constraint.typeParamIndex)
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticWhereConstraintViolation, message: "Type '{String.from(sourceType)}' does not satisfy constraint '{String.from(constraint.interfaceName)}' required by type parameter '{String.from(constrainedTypeParamName(signatures, baseName: baseName, position: constraint.typeParamIndex as FieldIndex))}' of '{String.from(baseName)}'", line: site.nameLine, column: site.nameColumn, filePath: site.filePath)
end 'reportUnsatisfiedConstraint'

// The NAME of the constrained type parameter — the `T` of `where T is Bar` — for the E3017 sentence, which
// the canonical corpus spells naming it (`specs/where-clauses.md`'s `and-violation`). It lives on the SAME
// layout `structWhereConstraints` read the constraint off, so the position is an index into that
// declaration's own `uses` list by construction.
//
// Read only on the VIOLATION path, and that is deliberate: `structOf` boxes a `DeclaredStruct` per call
// (a Maxon union value is a heap object in every case), and a conforming instantiation — every one in a
// program that compiles — must not pay for a diagnostic it does not produce.
function constrainedTypeParamName(signatures ProgramSignatures, baseName ByteArray, position FieldIndex) returns ByteArray
	match signatures.structInAnyFile(baseName) 'base'
		declared(layout) then return try layout.typeParamNames.get(position) otherwise panic("constrainedTypeParamName: '{String.from(baseName)}' constrains type-parameter position {position}, outside its own `uses` list of {layout.typeParamNames.count()} — a `where` constraint is recorded against that list")
		undeclared then panic("constrainedTypeParamName: '{String.from(baseName)}' carries a `where` constraint but is in no struct registry — `structWhereConstraints` read that constraint off the very layout this lookup misses")
	end 'base'
end 'constrainedTypeParamName'

// ⭐ **DOES `typeName` CONFORM TO `interfaceName`? THE ONE predicate every conformance site consults (P1.7a
// slice 2b + P1.7b).** An intrinsic builtin-type conformance (`int`/`String` implements `Hashable`/`Equatable`,
// synthesized natively) ORed with the user-declared registry — so "int conforms to Hashable" is decided in
// exactly one place, never split between an intrinsic answer and a registry answer that could drift. `typeName`
// is the CANONICAL conformer name (`conformerTypeArgName`); the registry is keyed by the declared struct name,
// which a ranged-int alias's `int` or a `String` argument's `String` never collides with, so the OR is
// unambiguous.
//
// ⚠ **`discovered` IS THE INTRINSIC TABLE'S TWO PROGRAM-KEYED ROWS, AND EVERY CALLER DERIVES IT THROUGH
// `ProgramSignatures.discoveredConformerOf` (ARR6, enum-hashable)** — never by comparing against a name of
// its own. The array row's key is the spelling the CORPUS declares the record under, discovered from the
// `BuiltinArrayLiteral` marker; the enum row's is whatever the author named their `enum`. Both are the same
// discovered answer `conformerNameOfType` already reduced `typeName` to. Asking one home means the reduction
// and the rows cannot come to disagree about which conformer a name is.
export function typeConformsTo(declared DeclaredConformanceMap, interfaces IrInterfaceMap, typeName ByteArray, interfaceName ByteArray, discovered DiscoveredConformer) returns bool
	if isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance(typeName, interfaceName: interfaceName, discovered: discovered) 'intrinsic'
		return true
	end 'intrinsic'
	return typeDeclaresInterface(declared, interfaces: interfaces, typeName: typeName, interfaceName: interfaceName)
end 'typeConformsTo'

// Which of a conditional TYPE extension's constraints a concrete receiver instance FAILS, or `met`. It names
// the interface and the offending type argument because those are the two nouns the refusal quotes.
export union ExtensionConstraintOnReceiver
	met
	unmet(interfaceName ByteArray, argDisplayName ByteArray)
end 'ExtensionConstraintOnReceiver'

// ⭐ **DOES A CONCRETE GENERIC INSTANCE SATISFY A CONDITIONAL TYPE EXTENSION'S CLAUSE?** — asked at the two
// points a call to such a method can be judged, and written ONCE because a refusal that fires at one and not
// the other is either a false refusal or a witness table built for a conformance nothing validated:
//
//   • `Parser.requireReceiverSatisfiesExtensionConstraints`, at the member token, which is where the
//     diagnostic reads best and is the only decider while the callee names ONE declaration;
//   • `SemanticCheck.checkResolvedExtensionConstraints`, on the RESOLVED overload member, which is the only
//     decider once the name is an overload SET — there the parser cannot know which declaration the call
//     means, so it defers (see `ProgramSignatures.hasUnconstrainedExtensionDeclaration`).
export function receiverMeetsExtensionConstraints(signatures ProgramSignatures, gid GenericInstanceId, constraints WhereConstraintArray) returns ExtensionConstraintOnReceiver
	for i in 0 upto constraints.count() 'eachConstraint'
		let constraint = try constraints.get(i) otherwise panic("receiverMeetsExtensionConstraints: constraints.get OOB at {i} below count {constraints.count()}")
		// The CANONICAL conformer name is what the conformance answer and the `__witness_<conformer>.*`
		// label are both filed under (`conformerTypeArgName`); the MESSAGE prints the source spelling, which
		// is `checkOneInstantiation`'s split applied at the other end of the same mechanism.
		let conformer = signatures.conformerTypeArgName(gid, position: constraint.typeParamIndex).toByteArray()
		if not typeConformsTo(signatures.sweptConformanceIndex(), interfaces: signatures.interfaceMap(), typeName: conformer, interfaceName: constraint.interfaceName, discovered: signatures.discoveredConformerOf(conformer)) 'violates'
			return ExtensionConstraintOnReceiver.unmet(constraint.interfaceName, argDisplayName: signatures.instanceArgDisplayName(gid, position: constraint.typeParamIndex))
		end 'violates'
	end 'eachConstraint'
	return ExtensionConstraintOnReceiver.met
end 'receiverMeetsExtensionConstraints'

// ⭐⭐ **HOW AN OPAQUE TYPE PARAMETER DISCHARGES A CONSTRAINT — the ONE answer, read by the CHECK that
// accepts the program and by the LOWERING that supplies the witness pointer (W41).**
//
// Under dictionary-passing a generic body is compiled ONCE over an opaque `T`, so a call it makes into
// another constrained generic binds that callee's constrained parameter to `T` itself. There is no concrete
// conformer to name, and the question stops being *"what does this type implement"*. It becomes **"does the
// declaration that OWNS this parameter already promise the interface — and in WHICH of its witness
// slots"**.
//
// ⭐ **ONE FUNCTION, BECAUSE IF THE TWO SIDES DRIFT NEITHER FAILURE IS A DIAGNOSTIC.**
// `checkOneInstantiation` decides whether the program is legal; `LowerMaxonToStd.materializeWitnessArg`
// decides which pointer is passed. Accept a shape the lowering cannot place and the compile PANICS inside
// lowering (MEASURED: `witnessSlotImpl: no conformance selected a member for slot 'T….Hashable.hash'`).
// Place a slot the check never verified and the callee reads a witness table out of a slot holding
// something else — a wild indirect call, which nothing downstream can notice. So ACCEPTANCE and INDEX are
// one answer computed once, the arrangement `typeExtensionWitnessConstraints` already has for the
// reservation and supply sides of the same ABI.
//
// ⭐ **THE INDEX IS INTO THE OWNER DECLARATION'S OWN `whereConstraints`, WHICH IS THE CALLER'S WITNESS
// BLOCK.** A plain method of a type carries exactly the type's constraint list in declaration order, and a
// type EXTENSION's method carries that list followed by its extras — the prefix property
// `forwardCallerWitness` rests on and `typeExtensionWitnessConstraints` exists to guarantee. So slot `i`
// here IS witness parameter `i` there. ⚠ The lowering re-checks that the enclosing function is a method of
// the owner declaration before forwarding, because that premise is the only thing between this index and a
// wrong register.
//
// ⚠ **`extends` COUNTS, through the same `conformanceSatisfiesInterface` a concrete conformer is asked
// about** — `where T is Ordered` with `interface Ordered extends Equatable` discharges a callee's
// `Equatable`, and it discharges it FROM THE `Ordered` SLOT, which is the table that callee needs.
module union OpaqueConstraintDischarge
	// The owner's witness slot that already carries this interface for this parameter.
	slot(index WitnessSlotIndex)
	// The owner declaration promises nothing reaching `interfaceName` — E3017, and never a forward.
	unsatisfied
end 'OpaqueConstraintDischarge'

module function opaqueParamDischarge(signatures ProgramSignatures, interfaces IrInterfaceMap, token TypeParamId, interfaceName ByteArray) returns OpaqueConstraintDischarge
	// A token no declaration filed owns nothing and promises nothing. `typeParamOwnerOf` THROWS on absence
	// rather than panicking, and answering `unsatisfied` here is the safe direction: a refused program, never
	// a forwarded slot nobody reserved.
	let owner = try signatures.typeParamOwnerOf(token) otherwise return OpaqueConstraintDischarge.unsatisfied
	let constraints = signatures.structWhereConstraints(owner.declaringTypeName)
	for i in 0 upto constraints.count() 'eachOwnerConstraint'
		let promised = try constraints.get(i) otherwise panic("opaqueParamDischarge: whereConstraints.get OOB at {i} for `type {String.from(owner.declaringTypeName)}`, whose own list this iterates")
		if promised.typeParamIndex != owner.position 'aboutAnotherParameter'
			continue
		end 'aboutAnotherParameter'

		if promised.interfaceName.equals(interfaceName) or conformanceSatisfiesInterface(interfaces, declaredName: promised.interfaceName, wanted: interfaceName) 'discharges'
			return OpaqueConstraintDischarge.slot(i)
		end 'discharges'
	end 'eachOwnerConstraint'

	return OpaqueConstraintDischarge.unsatisfied
end 'opaqueParamDischarge'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A CONSTRAINED TYPE PARAMETER'S OWN `where` CLAUSE CLAIMS AT `interfaceName`'s ASSOCIATED
// POSITIONS (G19)** — the constrained-parameter twin of `IrFunction.valueExistentialInstances`, which answers
// the same question for a receiver declared at a PARAMETERIZED EXISTENTIAL.
//
// `where Source is Iterator with Element` says the conformance behind `Source` binds `Iterator`'s `Element`
// to the enclosing declaration's own `Element`. That is the one fact a type parameter cannot carry in its
// type, and three tiers need it about the SAME receiver, so it is derived HERE once rather than at each:
// the parser types the dispatch's RESULT through it (`Parser.associatedReturnThroughConstraintBinding`),
// `dispatchedRequirementSlots` counts it as a site CLAIM so E3119 stops asking the conformers to agree about
// a position this dispatch settled, and the lowering types the associated FORMALS through it
// (`LowerMaxonToStd.associatedFormalType`). Two derivations would be a check that passes and an ABI that
// says something else.
//
// ⚠ **THE INTERFACE MUST MATCH EXACTLY — `conformanceSatisfiesInterface` IS THE WRONG QUESTION HERE.** The
// bindings are positions in THIS interface's own `uses` list, and associated types do not project through
// `extends` (`checkOneInterfaceConformance` records a derived interface with an empty `associatedTypeNames`),
// so a `where T is Derived with X` says nothing about a requirement declared on `Base`. `opaqueParamDischarge`
// one door up asks the wider question because a witness SLOT genuinely serves the parent; a BINDING does not.
//
// ⚠ It reads the declaring TYPE's own constraints (`structWhereConstraints`), which is `opaqueParamDischarge`'s
// scope exactly: a type EXTENSION's extra constraints are not in that list, so a binding written there claims
// nothing and the position stays E3119's to decide — a refusal, never a wrong ABI.
//
// The empty list is the answer for every unconstrained parameter and every constraint that binds nothing,
// which is the whole corpus but the shapes this exists for — and it is the SHARED empty, so the common path
// allocates nothing.
export function constrainedParamAssociatedBindings(signatures ProgramSignatures, token TypeParamId, interfaceName ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
	let owner = try signatures.typeParamOwnerOf(token) otherwise return sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances
	let constraints = signatures.structWhereConstraints(owner.declaringTypeName)
	for i in 0 upto constraints.count() 'eachOwnerConstraint'
		let promised = try constraints.get(i) otherwise panic("constrainedParamAssociatedBindings: whereConstraints.get OOB at {i} for `type {String.from(owner.declaringTypeName)}`, whose own list this iterates")
		if promised.typeParamIndex != owner.position or not promised.interfaceName.equals(interfaceName) 'anotherConstraint'
			continue
		end 'anotherConstraint'

		return promised.associatedBindings
	end 'eachOwnerConstraint'

	return sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances
end 'constrainedParamAssociatedBindings'

// Does this verdict discharge the constraint? The bool the union deliberately is not, for the one caller
// that needs the verdict WITHOUT the index — the E3017 check, which has a diagnostic to emit and no slot to
// place. Asked through this rather than by re-matching the union, so "discharged" has one definition.
function opaqueConstraintIsDischarged(verdict OpaqueConstraintDischarge) returns bool
	return match verdict 'verdict'
		slot gives true
		unsatisfied gives false
	end 'verdict'
end 'opaqueConstraintIsDischarged'

// The verdict on ONE conformer's bindings against ONE conditional extension's `where` clause. A CASE and not
// a bool, because the failing arm has to carry what the diagnostic names — which constraint, and what the
// conformer bound the constrained parameter to — and a caller must not be able to reach for those after a
// `satisfied`.
export union ExtensionConstraintCheck
	satisfied
	unmet(interfaceName ByteArray, bindingName ByteArray)
end 'ExtensionConstraintCheck'

// Does this verdict mean the extension gives the conformer NOTHING — no methods, and (W3) no nested
// typealiases either? The bool the union deliberately is not, for the one caller that needs the verdict
// WITHOUT the names: `Parser.foldExtensionDeclarationInto` publishes an extension body's aliases only for the
// conformers the extension actually reaches, and has no diagnostic to fill in. Asked through this rather
// than by re-matching the union, so "withheld" has one definition.
export function extensionMethodsWithheld(verdict ExtensionConstraintCheck) returns bool
	return match verdict 'verdict'
		satisfied gives false
		unmet gives true
	end 'verdict'
end 'extensionMethodsWithheld'

// What a conformer that binds NOTHING at a constrained position is reported as binding. It reaches the
// author only through the withheld-method diagnostic, and only on a program `checkOneInterfaceConformance`
// separately refuses (E3016 names the unbound associated type), so it is a phrase and never a type name.
let UnboundAssociatedTypePhrase = b"(nothing)"

// ⭐ **A CONFORMER'S `with` BINDINGS, OR NOTHING — THE POSITIONAL-PAIRING GATE (W43).** Every reader of
// `StructLayout.conformanceBindings` pairs it against the interface's associated-type names BY INDEX, so a
// list of a different LENGTH has no positional meaning at all: reading position `i` out of it answers about
// a neighbour rather than about the name it was asked for. Emptying it is what makes the two readers agree —
// a body parsed with no substitution, a `where` clause reported UNMET — instead of one going missing while
// the other goes wrong. See `Parser.conformerAssociatedBindings` for how the two lists come to disagree at
// all (the declaration sweep cannot know an interface's arity).
export function pairableConformanceBindings(bindings ByteArrayArray, associatedTypeNames ByteArrayArray) returns ByteArrayArray
	if bindings.count() != associatedTypeNames.count() 'noPositionalMeaning'
		return sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances
	end 'noPositionalMeaning'

	return bindings
end 'pairableConformanceBindings'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES `bindings` SATISFY A CONDITIONAL EXTENSION'S `where` CLAUSE? (conditional extensions)** —
// asked once per (extension, conformer) by `Parser.foldExtensionDeclarationInto`, whose answer decides
// whether the extension's methods exist on that conformer at all.
//
// The constraint's `typeParamIndex` is a position in the extended interface's `uses` list, and `bindings` is
// the conformer's `implements … with …` arguments in exactly that positional order
// (`StructLayout.conformanceBindings`), so the pairing is index arithmetic and not a search.
//
// ⚠ **THE BINDING IS REDUCED TO ITS CANONICAL CONFORMER NAME BEFORE THE QUESTION IS ASKED** — `Integer`
// reduces to `int`, which is the name its intrinsic `Equatable`/`Hashable` conformance and its
// `__witness_int.*` tables are filed under. Asking about the alias spelling instead would withhold
// `contains` from every `Array with <ranged alias>` in the program, which is the motivating case for the
// feature.
export function checkExtensionConstraints(signatures ProgramSignatures, constraints WhereConstraintArray, bindings ByteArrayArray) returns ExtensionConstraintCheck
	for i in 0 upto constraints.count() 'eachConstraint'
		let constraint = try constraints.get(i) otherwise panic("checkExtensionConstraints: constraints.get OOB at {i} below count {constraints.count()}")
		let binding = try bindings.get(constraint.typeParamIndex) otherwise return ExtensionConstraintCheck.unmet(constraint.interfaceName, bindingName: UnboundAssociatedTypePhrase)
		let conformer = signatures.conformerNameOfDeclaredName(binding).toByteArray()
		if not typeConformsTo(signatures.sweptConformanceIndex(), interfaces: signatures.interfaceMap(), typeName: conformer, interfaceName: constraint.interfaceName, discovered: signatures.discoveredConformerOf(conformer)) 'violates'
			return ExtensionConstraintCheck.unmet(constraint.interfaceName, bindingName: binding)
		end 'violates'
	end 'eachConstraint'

	return ExtensionConstraintCheck.satisfied
end 'checkExtensionConstraints'

// ⭐ **THE DECLARED-CONFORMANCE INDEX, BUILT ONCE PER COMPILE (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).** Three passes
// now ask "does this type conform?" — the E3017 where-constraint check, the `<Type> from "…"` check, and the
// EXISTENTIAL widening doors, which ask it once per interface-typed argument, assignment, field store and
// return in the program. Rebuilding the map at each of those would be O(sites x declarations); memoizing it
// on the `Project` beside the store it is derived from makes it one build and one lookup.
//
// ⚠ It resets in `resetMergeTargets`, with `conformances` — the store it is derived from — for
// `WitnessSlotImplMap`'s reason stated in full there: a warm rebuild whose sources changed which types
// declare what would otherwise answer from the PREVIOUS compile's index, and a stale "conforms" is a
// dispatch through a witness table nothing emitted.
export function declaredConformanceIndex(project Project) returns DeclaredConformanceMap
	if project.declaredConformanceIndexBuilt 'alreadyBuilt'
		return project.declaredConformances
	end 'alreadyBuilt'

	project.declaredConformances = buildDeclaredConformanceMap(project)
	project.declaredConformanceIndexBuilt = true
	return project.declaredConformances
end 'declaredConformanceIndex'

// ⭐⭐ **MAY A VALUE OF `valueType` STAND WHERE AN `interfaceRef` IS DECLARED? — THE WIDENING DOOR
// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).**
//
// ⚠ **ONE CALLER, BECAUSE AT THIS SLICE THERE IS ONE WIDENING POSITION:** the call ARGUMENT
// (`SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType`). A parameter is the only declared place with room for the fat pointer's
// second word — it travels as an adjacent hidden argument — so a `return`, a struct FIELD, a union payload
// and a container element are REFUSED outright at their declarations
// (`Parser.requireStorableDeclaredType`) rather than widened. v1 checks the argument too and lets its other
// three positions soft-fail to a NULL witness, which is a wild jump at the first dispatch; shv2 has no
// other position to soft-fail, and the day one opens it asks HERE rather than growing a second test.
//
// Two ways a value may stand there, and the second is the one that costs nothing:
//
//   • it is ALREADY an existential at the SAME interface, in which case the site FORWARDS the witness it
//     already carries and no table is looked up;
//   • it is a CONCRETE conformer, in which case the site materializes `__witness_<conformer>.<Iface>`.
//
// ⚠ **A DIFFERENT INTERFACE IS REFUSED, INCLUDING A DERIVED ONE, and that is a representation fact rather
// than a type-system opinion.** A `Derived` value's witness half addresses `__witness_<T>.Derived`, and a
// `Base`-typed place dispatches by numbering slots in `Base`'s OWN table — two different tables with two
// different slot orders. Forwarding one where the other is expected is a wrong function pointer, not a
// widening, and converting between them needs a runtime table the compiler does not emit. (v1 forwards it
// silently; `interfaceTypeIsFat` short-circuits before the witness is ever re-resolved. That is the bug this
// refusal is.) A CONCRETE `Impl implements Derived` reaching a `Base` place is a different question and is
// ALLOWED — `typeConformsTo` walks `extends`, and `__witness_Impl.Base` has a real impl in every slot.
export function valueWidensToInterface(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, valueType MaxonType, interfaceName ByteArray) returns bool
	if maxonTypeTag(valueType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'alreadyExistential'
		return interfaceNameOfType(valueType, typeNames: project.typeNames).equals(interfaceName)
	end 'alreadyExistential'

	let conformer = signatures.conformerNameOfType(valueType, idsFrom: project.typeNames).toByteArray()
	return typeConformsTo(declaredConformanceIndex(project), interfaces: project.interfaces, typeName: conformer, interfaceName: interfaceName, discovered: signatures.discoveredConformerOf(conformer))
end 'valueWidensToInterface'

// Build the DECLARED-conformance index from `project.conformances` — a type NAME to the set of interfaces it
// declares `implements`. The E3017 check's `Point declares Digest?` lookup, built once per constrained program.
function buildDeclaredConformanceMap(project Project) returns DeclaredConformanceMap
	var declared = DeclaredConformanceMap.create()
	for conformance in project.conformances 'eachDecl'
		var interfaces = InterfaceNameSet.create()
		copyDeclaredInterfaces(declared, typeName: conformance.typeName, into: interfaces)
		for i in 0 upto conformance.interfaces.count() 'eachIface'
			interfaces.insert((try conformance.interfaces.get(i) otherwise panic("buildDeclaredConformanceMap: interfaces.get OOB at {i}")).interfaceName)
		end 'eachIface'
		declared.upsert(conformance.typeName, value: interfaces)
	end 'eachDecl'
	return declared
end 'buildDeclaredConformanceMap'

// ⭐⭐ **A CONFORMER'S ENTRY IS THE UNION OF ITS DECLARATIONS, AND THE REPLACE THIS REPAIRS LET DECLARATION
// ORDER DECIDE WHICH CONFORMANCES A PROGRAM HAD.** The header this replaces argued that a fresh set per decl
// was enough because *"one `type X implements A, B` clause is ONE ConformanceDecl"* and a second `implements`
// on one type is E3006 — true of the `type` clause alone, and FALSE the moment an `extension` could add one:
// `Parser.recordExtensionConformance` records a SECOND `ConformanceDecl` under the same conformer name, which
// is not a redeclaration and is not E3006. MEASURED on the merge base, one file, two declarations:
// `type Thing implements Shower` plus `extension Thing implements Sizer` compiles the `Sizer` call and refuses
// the `Shower` one with *"E3005 … type 'Thing' does not implement interface 'Shower'"* — and swapping which
// is written first swaps which of the two is refused. The last decl won; every earlier one was dropped.
//
// ⚠ It is also load-bearing for the reachability walk that reads this map to credit a conformer's witness
// impls (`StdlibSource.markWitnessImplsOfConformer`): `stdlib/Array.maxon` declares `Iterable`/`Cloneable` on
// the type and `Hashable`/`Equatable` on an extension, so under the replace one of those pairs was invisible
// to the credit and its impls would be filed unreachable behind a witness slot that names them.
//
// ⚠ **THE SET IS COPIED RATHER THAN ADDED TO, for `recordDispatchedSlot`'s reason stated in full there** — a
// value fetched out of a `Map` BORROWS the map, so an `insert` on it is E3019 and an `upsert` while that
// borrow is live is E3070. The copy is paid once per DECLARATION and copies only what an earlier declaration
// of the SAME conformer already put there, which is nothing for every type in the corpus but the handful
// carrying both a `type` clause and an `extension` one.
function copyDeclaredInterfaces(declared DeclaredConformanceMap, typeName ByteArray, into InterfaceNameSet)
	if let already = try declared.get(typeName) 'declaredEarlierToo'
		for interfaceName in already 'eachEarlier'
			into.insert(interfaceName)
		end 'eachEarlier'
	end 'declaredEarlierToo'
end 'copyDeclaredInterfaces'

// Does `typeName` declare `implements interfaceName`, DIRECTLY OR THROUGH `extends` (P1.7a slice 2a; made
// transitive by P1.7a-existentials slice 1)? A type with no conformance declaration answers false — the E3017
// case.
//
// ⚠ **THE `extends` WALK IS WHY IT TAKES THE INTERFACE REGISTRY**, and the walk itself is
// `IrInterface.declaredInterfacesReach` — the ONE list-level home, shared with
// `ProgramSignatures.conformersOf`, which decides the same question for interface EXTENSIONS. What is left
// here is the only thing this door adds: the INDEX lookup, and the rule that a type with no row in it
// declares nothing. The two used to spell the direct-hit-then-walk orchestration separately and had already
// drifted on cost; see that function for the measurement.
//
// ⭐ Exported for `conformersOf`, which reaches it with `ProgramSignatures.sweptConformanceIndex()` — the
// same `type -> declared interfaces` relation this is handed, built from `StructLayout.conformsTo`.
export function typeDeclaresInterface(declared DeclaredConformanceMap, interfaces IrInterfaceMap, typeName ByteArray, interfaceName ByteArray) returns bool
	if let declaredInterfaces = try declared.get(typeName) 'hasDecl'
		return declaredInterfacesReach(interfaces, declaredNames: declaredInterfaces, wanted: interfaceName)
	end 'hasDecl'
	return false
end 'typeDeclaresInterface'

// Check one `type X implements I1, I2` declaration against each named interface.
//
// The conforming type's `uses` names are read ONCE per declaration rather than per rendered type: they
// depend only on the conforming type, and `project.structTypes` is a map lookup that would otherwise run
// per rendered parameter.
function checkOneConformance(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, conformance ConformanceDecl, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	// The conforming type's own `uses` names — the list a `typeParameter(token)` in one of its methods is
	// re-spelled against (`typeParamPositionIn`, W14: a token is a digest of `(declaring type, parameter)`,
	// not an index, so the position is recovered by re-spelling and never by subscripting this list), read
	// ONCE per declaration rather than per rendered type (it is a map lookup, and it does not vary).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THIS PARAGRAPH CLAIMED A SOLE PRODUCER AND WAS ALREADY WRONG ABOUT ITS OWN INPUT, WHICH IS WHAT
	// MADE THE PANIC BELOW A REACHABLE COMPILER ABORT (W133).** It read *"Only a `type` declaration records a
	// ConformanceDecl (`Parser.parseTypeDeclaration` is the sole caller of `parseTypeImplementsClause`)"* —
	// false since conditional extensions: `Parser.recordExtensionConformance` files one per CONFORMER of every
	// `extension X implements …`. It was harmless only because every conformer such an extension could reach
	// was a declared `type`, so the two producers happened to agree about what a conformer is. **W133 makes
	// `extension int implements Hashable` a program, and `int` is in no struct registry and never will be** —
	// a primitive has no fields, so there is nothing for a layout to describe. Left unnarrowed the check
	// aborted the compiler on a legal program that both references compile.
	//
	// ⚠ **NARROWED, NOT DELETED.** What it protects is real for every conformer that names a DECLARATION: a
	// `type` that recorded a conformance and committed no layout is a merge-invariant violation exactly as a
	// signature-less module function is. The primitive is the one conformer that legitimately has none, and it
	// is recognized by the SAME table (`primitiveTagOfTypeName`) that admitted it at the extension door, so
	// the two cannot come to disagree about which names those are.
	//
	// A primitive declares no `uses` parameters, which is also what a non-generic type's layout already holds
	// — so the empty list is not a fallback here, it is the answer.
	var typeParamNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
	if primitiveTagOfTypeName(conformance.typeName) == ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'aDeclaredConformer'
		let layout = try project.structTypes.get(conformance.typeName) otherwise panic("checkOneConformance: conforming type '{String.from(conformance.typeName)}' declares `implements` but is in no struct registry — a ConformanceDecl comes from a declared `type` (which commits a StructLayout) or from an `extension` over a primitive (which is not a declared type and was admitted above)")
		typeParamNames = layout.typeParamNames
	end 'aDeclaredConformer'

	for i in 0 upto conformance.interfaces.count() 'eachIface'
		let entry = try conformance.interfaces.get(i) otherwise panic("checkOneConformance: interfaces.get OOB at {i}")
		match lookupInterface(project.interfaces, name: entry.interfaceName) 'resolve'
			notFound then reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticUnknownInterface, message: "type '{String.from(conformance.typeName)}' implements unknown interface '{String.from(entry.interfaceName)}'", line: conformance.nameLine, column: conformance.nameColumn, filePath: conformance.filePath)
			found(iface) then checkOneInterfaceConformance(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: conformance, entry: entry, iface: iface, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)
		end 'resolve'
	end 'eachIface'
end 'checkOneConformance'

// ⭐ **EVERY ASSOCIATED TYPE THE INTERFACE DECLARES MUST BE BOUND, AND THAT IS ASKED BEFORE ANY SIGNATURE
// IS COMPARED (R5).** `type Broken implements HasElement` against `interface HasElement uses Element`
// leaves `Element` denoting nothing, so every requirement mentioning it would be reported as a WRONG
// SIGNATURE — the consequence of the mistake rather than the mistake, and a message telling the author to
// change a method that is already right. Both references diagnose it first and by name (bootstrap
// `2-Parser.cs:3401-3407`, v1 `TypeResolution.maxon:1161-1164`) and the spec pins the sentence.
//
// Bindings are POSITIONAL — `withArgs[i]` binds `associatedTypeNames[i]` — so "unbound" is exactly "at an
// index the argument list does not reach", and the FIRST such name is the one reported. Naming one rather
// than listing all of them is the references' shape, and it is the right one: a missing binding is written
// at a single site the author fixes in one edit.
//
// ⚖ **A SURPLUS ARGUMENT IS NOT DIAGNOSED *HERE*, AND AFTER R6 THAT IS A STATEMENT ABOUT THE TIER RATHER
// THAN ABOUT THE RULE.** The rule now splits on the SPELLING — `implements One with (Integer, Float)` against
// `interface One uses A` is refused (**E2066**, `Parser.checkParenthesizedConformanceArgs`), while the same
// surplus written WITHOUT parentheses is still ignored. That split cannot be made at this tier and never
// could: `InterfaceConformance` carries a `ByteArrayArray`, so by the time a conformance reaches this
// function the parentheses are gone and the two spellings are one value. Whether the list was written
// explicitly is a fact only the token stream holds, which is why the refusal lives in the parser.
//
// The two references disagree about the surplus, and shv2 takes NEITHER wholesale. The bootstrap binds
// `min(names, args)` and ignores the rest (`ResolveWithTypeParams`, `2-Parser.cs:2947-2953`); v1 rejects an
// arity mismatch in either direction (`TypeResolution.maxon:1152-1168`, as an E3015). shv2 keeps the
// bootstrap's silence for the UNPARENTHESIZED form, for two reasons and not as a coin flip:
//
//   • ONE rule then covers both shapes a conformance can get wrong. An argument list that is too SHORT is a
//     NAMED missing binding whatever its length, so `Pair with Integer` against `uses First, Second` says
//     `Second` — where v1 reaches its arity arm and reports a count instead, needing a second wording.
//     **This half is UNCHANGED by R6**: a deficit is still reported by name, in either spelling, right below.
//   • Unparenthesized, the LENGTH is not the author's assertion — it is decided by the interface's arity,
//     and a trailing comma legitimately belongs to the outer `implements` list (which is why both references
//     consume exactly `arity` items there, and why `surplus-conformance-argument-is-ignored` pins it).
//     Parenthesized, the length IS asserted, and an assertion can be wrong.
//
// ⚠ **THE INHERITED-`uses` ARGUMENT SURVIVES R6, NARROWED.** `interface Sub extends Base` where
// `Base uses Element` leaves `Sub` with an empty `associatedTypeNames` here, so `implements Sub with Score`
// — a shape v1 supports outright (`findInheritedBindings`) — has "too many" arguments against an interface
// that transitively requires exactly that one. That program is REJECTED TODAY EITHER WAY, and only v1 is the
// outlier: shv2 AND the bootstrap give the identical sentence, character for character,
// `E3016 … get() returns Integer (expected get() returns Element)`, because shv2 does not inherit the
// binding either. So R6 changes which sentence the parenthesized spelling gets, never accept into reject.
// The forward-compatibility obligation is recorded where it can be acted on — `Parser.conformanceUsesArity`,
// which also states why the transitive count cannot simply be walked at parse time today.
//
// ⚠ **W60 CHANGES THE NEIGHBOURING CASE AND DELIBERATELY NOT THIS ONE.** An inherited `uses` name is now
// bound when the SAME `implements` clause also conforms to the interface that declared it
// (`implements Cursor with Pos, BiCursor`); it is still bound by nothing when the clause writes the
// argument on the DESCENDANT instead (`implements BiCursor with Pos`), which is the shape above and which
// both references also refuse. See `collectInterfaceMethods` for the measurement that separates them.
function checkOneInterfaceConformance(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, conformance ConformanceDecl, entry InterfaceConformance, iface IrInterface, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	if entry.withArgs.count() < iface.associatedTypeNames.count() 'unboundAssociatedType'
		let unbound = try iface.associatedTypeNames.get(entry.withArgs.count()) otherwise panic("checkOneInterfaceConformance: associatedTypeNames.get OOB at {entry.withArgs.count()} below count {iface.associatedTypeNames.count()}")
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticPartialInterfaceImpl, message: "Type '{String.from(conformance.typeName)}' does not define required associated type '{String.from(unbound)}' from interface '{String.from(entry.interfaceName)}'", line: conformance.nameLine, column: conformance.nameColumn, filePath: conformance.filePath)
		return
	end 'unboundAssociatedType'

	checkInterfaceMethods(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: conformance, entry: entry, iface: iface, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)
end 'checkOneInterfaceConformance'

// Validate that a conforming type supplies every method the interface requires (its own AND its
// `extends`-inherited), each with a matching signature. Reports E3016 with the MISSING methods, else with
// the WRONG-SIGNATURE ones — missing first and mutually exclusive, mirroring the oracle's throw-on-first.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A `static` REQUIREMENT IS A REQUIREMENT (R9), AND SKIPPING IT WAS A REACHABLE COMPILER PANIC.**
// This loop used to `continue` on `method.isStatic`, so a conformer that supplied nothing for
// `static function tag()` was accepted — while `ensureWitnessTable` still reserved that method's slot and
// stamped a `funcAbs64InRdata` relocation naming `Point.tag`, a symbol no function installs. Two components
// disagreeing about whether the requirement exists, and the disagreement surfaced as
// `bakeFuncAbs64Relocs: … references .text function 'Point.tag', which is in no debug symbol`. The witness
// slot is the reason the skip cannot simply be narrowed to "only when a table is built": whether a table
// HAPPENS to be built must not decide whether a type conforms, so the check is unconditional and a program
// with no generic instantiation is now rejected too.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO REFERENCES DISAGREE HERE, AND v1 IS THE ONE shv2 FOLLOWS — HALFWAY.** The bootstrap
// `continue`s before its `func == null` test (`2-Parser.cs:3428-3432`), so a MISSING static is silently
// accepted; v1 pushes the missing entry first and only then skips (`SemanticCheck.maxon:412-430`), so a
// missing static IS reported. shv2 takes v1's answer for the missing case and diverges from BOTH for the
// present one: v1 skips a present static's SIGNATURE because *"no runtime witness is dispatched against
// them"*, which is exactly the sentence shv2's dictionary-passing falsifies — the table carries one slot per
// interface method, statics included, so a static whose shape disagrees with the requirement is an ABI
// disagreement in a slot, the same argument `validateThrowsConformance` already makes for shv2's own
// throws rules. The skip's original comment was right about WHY it existed (`checkOneMethod` reads an
// INSTANCE method) and wrong about how far that reaches: the fix is to make the lookup receiver-aware
// (`signatureMatches`' staticness test), not to stop asking.
function checkInterfaceMethods(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, conformance ConformanceDecl, entry InterfaceConformance, iface IrInterface, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	let collected = collectInterfaceMethods(project, signatures: signatures, conformance: conformance, entry: entry, iface: iface, typeParamNames: typeParamNames)

	var missing = StringArray.create()
	var wrongSignature = StringArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto collected.count() 'eachMethod'
		let collectedMethod = try collected.get(i) otherwise panic("checkInterfaceMethods: collected.get OOB at {i}")
		checkOneMethod(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, rootInterfaceName: entry.interfaceName, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures, missing: missing, wrongSignature: wrongSignature)
	end 'eachMethod'

	if missing.count() > 0 'hasMissing'
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticPartialInterfaceImpl, message: "Partial interface implementation: type '{String.from(conformance.typeName)}' is missing {missing.count()} method(s):\n{joinBulletedLines(missing)}", line: conformance.nameLine, column: conformance.nameColumn, filePath: conformance.filePath)
		return
	end 'hasMissing'

	if wrongSignature.count() > 0 'hasWrongSignature'
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticPartialInterfaceImpl, message: "Partial interface implementation: type '{String.from(conformance.typeName)}' has {wrongSignature.count()} method(s) with wrong signature:\n{joinBulletedLines(wrongSignature)}", line: conformance.nameLine, column: conformance.nameColumn, filePath: conformance.filePath)
	end 'hasWrongSignature'
end 'checkInterfaceMethods'

// The interface's requirements, transitively through `extends`, each SUBSTITUTED under what this
// conformance bound the associated types of the interface that DECLARED it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE WALK ITSELF IS `IrInterface.interfaceWitnessSlots` AND IS NO LONGER WRITTEN HERE (R10c).** It
// answers "which requirement is slot `i` of this interface?", and the witness-table BUILDER and the
// dispatch RESOLVER ask the identical question — so the requirement list a conformance is checked against
// and the slot list a table is laid out by are ONE answer. They were not: this walk had the `extends` chain
// and the other two did not, which is the whole of R10c. Order, the diamond rule and the cyclic-chain guard
// are stated there, once.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHAT STAYS HERE IS THE SUBSTITUTION, AND IT IS THE ONLY PLACE ASSOCIATED TYPES ARE SUBSTITUTED (R5).** A
// collected requirement is ALREADY substituted, so the two things that read one — `signatureMatches`, which
// decides the verdict, and `formatInterfaceMethodSignature`, which writes the sentence — are reading one
// string rather than each deriving their own. That is v1's shape (`substituteMethodSig` builds
// `expectedMethods` once, `TypeResolution.maxon:1253+`, and `checkConformanceForType` +
// `formatMethodSig` both read it) and deliberately NOT the bootstrap's, which substitutes twice off one
// dict — `ResolveInterfaceTypeName` inside `SignatureMatches` and `FormatResolved` at the message
// (`2-Parser.cs:3577`, `MlirType.cs`). Under the two-site shape a substitution that reaches the comparison
// and misses the message yields a CORRECT VERDICT WITH A WRONG SENTENCE, which no positive test can see —
// which is why `error.extends.wrong-signature-under-an-inherited-binding-names-the-substituted-type` asserts
// the SENTENCE of a still-rejected program and not merely its code.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A REQUIREMENT IS SUBSTITUTED UNDER TWO PAIRS, NOT ONE, AND THE SECOND IS W60's WHOLE CONTENT.**
// The CLAUSE pair is the entry the walk started from — the pre-existing rule, and it is the one that
// survives verbatim: an `extends` parent that RE-DECLARES the same `uses` NAME is substituted by it,
// because substitution is by name and that spelling is the only vocabulary the two interfaces share
// (`interface Derived extends Base uses Element`, which
// `associated-types.extends-projected-associated-type-single-conformer` writes and which a
// declaring-interface-only rule REGRESSES — measured, mid-rung, as
// `E3016 … take(e float) (expected take(e Element))`).
//
// The DECLARING pair is consulted for what the clause pair does not spell: a name introduced by an
// ANCESTOR that the root interface does not re-declare is bound by this same type's conformance to that
// ancestor. `Element` is *Cursor's* `uses` name, so what it denotes is what `implements … Cursor with Pos`
// bound it to — whichever entry of the clause wrote that, and in whatever order.
//
// ⚠ **CLAUSE FIRST, DECLARER SECOND, AND THE ORDER IS NOT ARBITRARY.** When both spell one name they are
// the same conformance seen from two ends, except in the one shape E3111 exists for —
// `implements Child with Whole, Parent with Real`, where they DISAGREE. Taking the clause's answer is what
// keeps the two routes to `Parent`'s slot comparing DIFFERENT requirements, which is what lets them select
// different members and be caught; declarer-first collapses both routes onto one binding and E3111 can no
// longer fire. That is also the whole reason this is a two-pair lookup rather than the bootstrap's single
// clause-wide dictionary, which cannot represent the disagreement at all.
//
// ⛔ **THE MISSING HALF WAS INVISIBLE BECAUSE TWO INDIVIDUALLY-CORRECT RULES NEVER MET.**
// `Parser.conformanceUsesArity` counts an interface's OWN `uses` names and deliberately not its inherited
// ones, so for `implements … BiCursor` the root's `associatedTypeNames` is EMPTY and the guard below took
// the "nothing to substitute" path — while the slot list it hands back has, since R10c, included every
// requirement `Cursor` declares. MEASURED: `type Walker implements Cursor with Pos, BiCursor` was
// `E3016 … current() returns Pos (expected current() returns Element)`, and striking `, BiCursor` — which
// REMOVES a conformance — made the same program compile and print 7. Conforming to a DESCENDANT destroyed
// the binding of its ANCESTOR. The bootstrap accepts both spellings (it merges the whole clause into one
// dictionary); shv2 keeps the entries apart, which is what lets E3111 tell two routes to one slot apart, so
// the join has to be made HERE, per slot, rather than by flattening the clause.
//
// ⚖ **AND THE ABSENCE OF A SIBLING ENTRY BINDS NOTHING — MEASURED AGAINST BOTH REFERENCES, NOT ASSUMED.**
// `implements BiCursor` alone and `implements BiCursor with Pos` are BOTH rejected by the bootstrap with the
// identical `expected get() returns Element` sentence shv2 already gives them, so neither an inherited-arity
// rule (v1's `findInheritedBindings`, which shv2 does not have — see `Parser.conformanceUsesArity`) nor a
// silent default belongs here: a name no entry of this clause binds is left denoting ITSELF, which is what
// `error.inherited-uses-name-unparenthesized-is-still-a-signature-error` pins and what this rung must not move.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND AN INTERFACE-SCOPED `typealias` IS EXPANDED AT THIS SAME DOOR (W61).** `function absorb(value
// ElementArray)` inside `interface Holder uses Element` renders its parameter as the unresolved name
// `Holder.ElementArray`, while every conformer's own alias renders a RESOLVED `Array_…` — so the two sides
// of one requirement were compared as an unresolved name against an instance and every conformer was
// refused. Expansion belongs HERE and not at a second site for R5's reason above: the expanded name is what
// the verdict compares AND what the diagnostic prints.
//
// ⚠ **EXPAND FIRST, THEN SUBSTITUTE — the order is not a preference, the reverse is undefined.** Before
// expansion the requirement mentions no associated type at all: `Holder.ElementArray` is one opaque name.
// It is the right-hand side that exposes `Element`, and the bound spellings the expansion is handed are
// produced by `boundTypeName` — the same two-pair lookup every other position uses — so the two rules
// compose rather than each having its own idea of what a name means.
//
// ⚠ **THE FAST PATH IS TWO CONDITIONS NOW, AND BOTH ARE EXACT.** "This clause binds nothing" has to
// replace "the ROOT interface declares no associated type", which is precisely the wrong question after the
// W60 paragraph: asking whether ANY entry wrote a `with` argument is one pass over a handful of entries,
// allocates nothing, needs no interface lookup, and is sufficient by construction, since every binding this
// function can apply comes from one of those lists. But a clause that binds nothing can still need
// EXPANSION — `interface Sink` with `typealias Bytes = Array with Byte` and no `uses` at all was equally
// broken (measured) — so `anySlotDeclarerHasAnAlias` rides beside it. That one is asked PER DECLARING
// INTERFACE and not program-wide, for the reason its own header gives: `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declares an
// alias, so a program-wide bool is true for every program that loads the stdlib and would put every
// conformance in the corpus back on the substituting path. Both conditions fail for every conformance
// outside this rung's cases and the two stdlib literal-init conformers, and the slot list is handed
// straight back.
function collectInterfaceMethods(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, conformance ConformanceDecl, entry InterfaceConformance, iface IrInterface, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray) returns CollectedInterfaceMethodArray
	let slots = interfaceWitnessSlots(project.interfaces, iface: iface)
	if conformanceBindsNothing(conformance) and not anySlotDeclarerHasAnAlias(signatures, slots: slots) 'nothingToSubstituteOrExpand'
		return slots
	end 'nothingToSubstituteOrExpand'

	let clauseNames = pairableAssociatedTypeNames(iface.associatedTypeNames, boundTypeNames: entry.withArgs)
	var substituted = CollectedInterfaceMethodArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto slots.count() 'eachSlot'
		let slot = try slots.get(i) otherwise panic("collectInterfaceMethods: slots.get OOB at {i} below count {slots.count()}")
		let ownerNames = associatedTypeNamesDeclaredBy(project, iface: iface, interfaceName: slot.sourceInterfaceName)
		let declaredArgs = boundArgumentsFor(conformance, entry: entry, interfaceName: slot.sourceInterfaceName)
		let under = RequirementSubstitution.create(signatures, clauseNames: clauseNames, clauseArgs: entry.withArgs, declaredNames: pairableAssociatedTypeNames(ownerNames, boundTypeNames: declaredArgs), declaredArgs: declaredArgs, ownerNames: ownerNames, ownerInterfaceName: slot.sourceInterfaceName, conformerName: conformance.typeName, conformerTypeParams: typeParamNames)
		substituted.push(CollectedInterfaceMethod.create(substituteAssociatedTypes(slot.method, under: under), sourceInterfaceName: slot.sourceInterfaceName))
	end 'eachSlot'

	return substituted
end 'collectInterfaceMethods'

// ⭐⭐ **EVERYTHING ONE REQUIREMENT IS SUBSTITUTED UNDER, IN ONE VALUE.** It replaced eight parallel
// parameters threaded through three functions, which is the shape that lets a caller pass the CLAUSE pair
// where the DECLARER's belongs and be caught by nothing. One is built per SLOT, and only on the path the
// fast path above did not take — a program that neither binds nor declares an interface alias allocates none.
type RequirementSubstitution
	// The entry the walk started from, and this type's own conformance entry for the interface that DECLARED
	// the requirement. Asked in that order — see `collectInterfaceMethods` for why clause-first is a rule.
	export let clauseNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let clauseArgs as ByteArrayArray
	export let declaredNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let declaredArgs as ByteArrayArray
	// ⚠ The declaring interface's `uses` names UNGATED by `pairableAssociatedTypeNames`, unlike
	// `declaredNames` above. The alias expansion pairs them against `boundTypeName`'s answers, which is
	// defined for every name whether or not an argument list could pair with it — where the substitution
	// proper indexes an argument list directly and must not be handed a name it cannot reach.
	export let ownerNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let ownerInterfaceName as ByteArray
	export let signatures as ProgramSignatures
	export let conformerName as ByteArray
	export let conformerTypeParams as ByteArrayArray

	export static function create(signatures ProgramSignatures, clauseNames ByteArrayArray, clauseArgs ByteArrayArray, declaredNames ByteArrayArray, declaredArgs ByteArrayArray, ownerNames ByteArrayArray, ownerInterfaceName ByteArray, conformerName ByteArray, conformerTypeParams ByteArrayArray) returns RequirementSubstitution
		return Self{clauseNames: clauseNames, clauseArgs: clauseArgs, declaredNames: declaredNames, declaredArgs: declaredArgs, ownerNames: ownerNames, ownerInterfaceName: ownerInterfaceName, signatures: signatures, conformerName: conformerName, conformerTypeParams: conformerTypeParams}
	end 'create'
end 'RequirementSubstitution'

// Could any requirement in this slot list spell an interface-scoped `typealias`? A requirement can only
// name an alias its OWN interface declared, so the question is exactly "does any interface that declared
// one of these requirements declare an alias" — a set membership per slot, and no allocation.
//
// ⚠ **THE WHOLE-PROGRAM BOOL IS ASKED FIRST AND IS NOT SUFFICIENT ALONE.** `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`
// declares `InitableFromArrayLiteral`'s `typealias ElementArray`, so it is TRUE for every program that
// loads the stdlib — which is every program. It buys the O(1) exit for a program with no interface alias
// at all; the per-slot walk is what keeps every OTHER conformance off the substituting path, where each
// requirement would otherwise cost a rebuilt slot record and a `RequirementSubstitution`.
function anySlotDeclarerHasAnAlias(signatures ProgramSignatures, slots CollectedInterfaceMethodArray) returns bool
	if not signatures.declaresInterfaceAliases() 'noneInTheProgram'
		return false
	end 'noneInTheProgram'

	for slot in slots 'eachSlot'
		if signatures.interfaceDeclaresAlias(slot.sourceInterfaceName) 'itsDeclarerHasOne'
			return true
		end 'itsDeclarerHasOne'
	end 'eachSlot'

	return false
end 'anySlotDeclarerHasAnAlias'

// Did this `implements` clause write a `with` argument anywhere? A clause that wrote none can bind nothing,
// whichever interface declared a requirement — the allocation-free gate above.
function conformanceBindsNothing(conformance ConformanceDecl) returns bool
	for candidate in conformance.interfaces 'eachEntry'
		if candidate.withArgs.count() != 0 'writesABinding'
			return false
		end 'writesABinding'
	end 'eachEntry'

	return true
end 'conformanceBindsNothing'

// The `uses` names `interfaceName` DECLARES — the list a requirement it declared is substituted against.
// The root interface is answered from the declaration already in hand; a parent costs the one lookup
// `interfaceWitnessSlots` itself already made to reach that slot, and only on a clause that binds something.
//
// A `notFound` is unreachable rather than tolerated: this name came off a slot the walk produced, and the
// walk produces a slot only for an interface it RESOLVED through this same door.
function associatedTypeNamesDeclaredBy(project Project, iface IrInterface, interfaceName ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
	if interfaceName.equals(iface.name) 'itsOwnRequirement'
		return iface.associatedTypeNames
	end 'itsOwnRequirement'

	match lookupInterface(project.interfaces, name: interfaceName) 'declaring'
		found(declaring) then return declaring.associatedTypeNames
		notFound then panic("associatedTypeNamesDeclaredBy: interface '{String.from(interfaceName)}' declared a requirement collected for '{String.from(iface.name)}' but no longer resolves — `interfaceWitnessSlots` reached it through this same registry moments ago")
	end 'declaring'
end 'associatedTypeNamesDeclaredBy'

// What THIS `implements` clause bound `interfaceName`'s `uses` names to: the entry the walk started from
// when that is the declaring interface, else the clause's own entry for it — searched, because
// `implements BiCursor, Cursor with Pos` writes the two in either order and the bootstrap accepts both
// (measured). The FIRST matching entry settles it, the order `checkTypeSymbolNamespace` and
// `AssociatedAbiBinding.disagreed` already report a contested claim in; a clause that names one interface
// twice with different arguments is rejected either way, and where the two selections MEET it is E3111.
function boundArgumentsFor(conformance ConformanceDecl, entry InterfaceConformance, interfaceName ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
	if interfaceName.equals(entry.interfaceName) 'theEntryBeingChecked'
		return entry.withArgs
	end 'theEntryBeingChecked'

	for candidate in conformance.interfaces 'eachEntry'
		if candidate.interfaceName.equals(interfaceName) 'conformsToTheDeclaringInterface'
			return candidate.withArgs
		end 'conformsToTheDeclaringInterface'
	end 'eachEntry'

	return sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances
end 'boundArgumentsFor'

// ⛔ **A NAME LIST WITH NO ARGUMENT LIST TO PAIR AGAINST BINDS NOTHING — AND IT IS THE *NAMES* THAT MUST BE
// EMPTIED, NOT THE ARGUMENTS.** `boundTypeName` is driven by the NAME list, so a short argument list left
// beside a full name list is not "no substitution", it is a subscript past the end of the arguments:
// MEASURED as `panic: boundTypeName: boundTypeNames.get OOB at 0 below associated-type count 1` on the
// harness's own `refusal/runtime-name-parsed-from-source` fixture, from the first cut of this rung which
// emptied the arguments instead. `pairableConformanceBindings` states the same positional rule for the
// registry copy of these lists, and states why a list of a different length has no positional meaning at all.
//
// The gate is a no-op for the entry being checked — `checkOneInterfaceConformance` reported a short one and
// returned before any requirement was collected — and it is what makes it true for a SIBLING entry too,
// which has had no such gate yet (its own check reports E3016 separately). Stating it HERE rather than
// relying on the remote guarantee is what turns `boundTypeName`'s panic into an unreachable invariant.
function pairableAssociatedTypeNames(associatedTypeNames ByteArrayArray, boundTypeNames ByteArrayArray) returns ByteArrayArray
	return sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances if boundTypeNames.count() < associatedTypeNames.count() else associatedTypeNames
end 'pairableAssociatedTypeNames'

// One requirement with every associated type replaced by what the conformance bound it to (R5) — the
// substitution the header above says happens exactly once. The two pairs are threaded as four arrays rather
// than merged into one, because merging them is not free of meaning: their lengths are what pairs them
// positionally, so a concatenation would have to be two arrays anyway, and it would allocate a pair per
// requirement of every binding conformance to say what four already-built lists say.
function substituteAssociatedTypes(method InterfaceMethodSig, under RequirementSubstitution) returns InterfaceMethodSig
	var paramTypeNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto method.paramTypeNames.count() 'eachParam'
		paramTypeNames.push(boundTypeName(try method.paramTypeNames.get(i) otherwise panic("substituteAssociatedTypes: paramTypeNames.get OOB at {i}"), under: under))
	end 'eachParam'

	return InterfaceMethodSig.create(method.name, paramNames: method.paramNames, paramTypeNames: paramTypeNames, returnTypeName: boundTypeName(method.returnTypeName, under: under), isStatic: method.isStatic, throwsClause: substituteThrowsClause(method.throwsClause, under: under), paramIsBuffer: method.paramIsBuffer)
end 'substituteAssociatedTypes'

// What ONE written type name in a requirement denotes under this conformance's bindings: the argument bound
// at the associated type's position, or the name itself when neither pair spells it. The CLAUSE pair is
// asked first and the DECLARING interface's second — see `collectInterfaceMethods` for why that order is a
// rule and not a preference. The scan that decides WHICH `uses` parameter a name is lives in
// `IrInterface.namePositionIn` — one statement of it, because the witness-call lowering asks the same
// question for a different reason (see there).
function boundTypeName(name ByteArray, under RequirementSubstitution) returns ByteArray
	let bound = boundAssociatedTypeName(name, under: under)
	if not bound.equals(name) 'anAssociatedTypeOfEitherPair'
		return bound
	end 'anAssociatedTypeOfEitherPair'

	// ⭐ **A NAME NEITHER PAIR SPELLS MAY STILL BE THE DECLARING INTERFACE'S OWN `typealias` (W61).** It is
	// asked LAST because the lookup above is what its own expansion is defined in terms of: an associated type
	// resolves to a binding, and only then does the alias's right-hand side mean anything. An interface that
	// declares no alias, and a name that is not one of its members, fall straight through to the name itself —
	// which is every requirement in the corpus outside this rung's cases.
	return try expandedInterfaceAlias(name, under: under) otherwise return name
end 'boundTypeName'

// ⛔ **THE ASSOCIATED-TYPE HALF ALONE, AND IT IS A SEPARATE FUNCTION TO MAKE THE RECURSION IMPOSSIBLE RATHER
// THAN MERELY UNREACHED.** The expansion below resolves the declaring interface's `uses` names through this
// door, and the first cut of this rung had it call `boundTypeName` — whose last act is to expand. Every name
// that reached the expansion therefore re-entered it, rebuilding the same spelling list for ever: **MEASURED
// as `panic: stack overflow … in Compiler.expandedInterfaceAlias / in Compiler.boundTypeName`, on all four
// W61 shapes at once.** That cut's header argued the recursion was bounded "by construction" because an
// associated type is found by one of the two pair lookups — an argument true only while both pairs are
// non-empty, which `pairableAssociatedTypeNames` exists to make false. A structure that CANNOT recurse needs
// no such argument.
function boundAssociatedTypeName(name ByteArray, under RequirementSubstitution) returns ByteArray
	if let clausePosition = try namePositionIn(under.clauseNames, name: name) 'boundByTheClause'
		return try under.clauseArgs.get(clausePosition) otherwise panic("boundAssociatedTypeName: clauseArgs.get OOB at {clausePosition} below name count {under.clauseNames.count()} — `pairableAssociatedTypeNames` empties the NAME list whenever the arguments cannot pair with it positionally, so a name reaching this point always has an argument at its own index")
	end 'boundByTheClause'

	if let declaredPosition = try namePositionIn(under.declaredNames, name: name) 'boundByItsDeclarer'
		return try under.declaredArgs.get(declaredPosition) otherwise panic("boundAssociatedTypeName: declaredArgs.get OOB at {declaredPosition} below name count {under.declaredNames.count()} — the same `pairableAssociatedTypeNames` gate applies to the declaring interface's pair")
	end 'boundByItsDeclarer'

	return name
end 'boundAssociatedTypeName'

// The alias's right-hand side under this conformance's bindings, as the canonical instance name the
// conformer's own declared parameter renders to. The bound spellings are the ASSOCIATED-TYPE lookup's answers
// for the declaring interface's `uses` names, which is what makes the expansion and the substitution ONE rule
// rather than two: whatever `Element` means in a requirement's parameter position is what it means inside the
// alias.
function expandedInterfaceAlias(name ByteArray, under RequirementSubstitution) returns ByteArray throws MapError
	var boundSpellings = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for ownerName in under.ownerNames 'eachAssociatedType'
		boundSpellings.push(boundAssociatedTypeName(ownerName, under: under))
	end 'eachAssociatedType'

	return try under.signatures.expandInterfaceAlias(name, associatedTypeNames: under.ownerNames, boundSpellings: boundSpellings, conformerName: under.conformerName, conformerTypeParams: under.conformerTypeParams, declaringInterfaceName: under.ownerInterfaceName)
end 'expandedInterfaceAlias'

// The `throws E` clause under the same bindings. It substitutes for the reason every other position does —
// `function next() throws Element` is as much an associated-type USE as a parameter is, and the throws
// relation is compared by NAME (`requireSameErrorType`), so an unsubstituted clause would reject a
// conforming impl. v1 substitutes it too, and says why in one line (`substituteThrowsName`,
// `TypeResolution.maxon:1249-1252`).
function substituteThrowsClause(clause ThrowsClause, under RequirementSubstitution) returns ThrowsClause
	match clause 'declared'
		none then return ThrowsClause.none
		throwing(name) then return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(boundTypeName(name.toByteArray(), under: under)))
	end 'declared'
end 'substituteThrowsClause'

// Check ONE interface method against the conforming type: absent -> `missing`, present but mismatched ->
// `wrongSignature`. A missing method's expected signature is tagged `(from Parent)` when it was inherited.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A REQUIREMENT IS MATCHED AGAINST EVERY MEMBER DECLARED UNDER ITS NAME, NOT AGAINST WHICHEVER ONE
// HOLDS THE BARE KEY (R10).** Under D7 only the FIRST declaration of a name registers as `Type.method`; a
// later overload is `Type.method#Suffix`. A lookup by the bare name alone therefore made CONFORMANCE DEPEND
// ON MEMBER DECLARATION ORDER — `label()` beside `label(extra Code)` satisfied `Named.label()` when it was
// written first and was rejected as a wrong signature when it was written second, with a message naming the
// member that is not even the candidate.
//
// ⚠ **THE OVERLOADED CASE IS SPLIT OFF AND PAYS FOR ITSELF ALONE, and that is measured rather than tidy.**
// Materializing a candidate list and a match list for EVERY requirement would allocate two arrays per
// conforming method of every program that declares any conformance — the same per-conforming-method cost
// `validateThrowsConformance`'s header records scale-test reading straight off a 16-byte union box
// (+16 … +264 allocations across the six rungs). A name that is not in `overloadSets` — every method in the
// corpus outside this rung's cases — takes the arm below and allocates nothing it did not allocate before.
function checkOneMethod(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, rootInterfaceName ByteArray, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures, missing StringArray, wrongSignature StringArray)
	let method = collectedMethod.method
	let qualifiedName = qualifiedMethodName(conformance.typeName, methodName: method.name)

	if let members = try project.overloadSets.get(qualifiedName) 'overloaded'
		checkOverloadedMembers(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, rootInterfaceName: rootInterfaceName, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures, members: members, missing: missing, wrongSignature: wrongSignature)
		return
	end 'overloaded'

	if let implFunc = try functionsByName.funcNamed(qualifiedName) 'present'
		let implSig = conformanceImplSignature(project, name: qualifiedName)
		if not signatureMatches(project, method: method, conformingType: conformance.typeName, implFunc: implFunc, implSig: implSig, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures) 'mismatch'
			wrongSignature.push("{formatActualSignature(project, methodName: method.name, conformingType: conformance.typeName, implFunc: implFunc, implSig: implSig, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)} (expected {formatInterfaceMethodSignature(method)})")
		end 'mismatch' else 'signatureOk'
			acceptConformingMember(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, selected: qualifiedName, implFunc: implFunc, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)
		end 'signatureOk'
		return
	end 'present'

	pushMissingRequirement(missing, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, rootInterfaceName: rootInterfaceName)
end 'checkOneMethod'

// The requirement's name is OVERLOADED: match it against every live member of the set and accept it when
// EXACTLY ONE matches (R10).
//
// ⚠ **EXACTLY ONE, because a witness slot holds exactly one address.** Two members can genuinely match one
// requirement — an overload set whose members differ only in a parameter NAME, or in a type the renderer
// spells alike — and taking the first would be the bare key's order-dependence restored under a different
// name. The call-site resolver already refuses that same pair as E3007, so refusing it here says the same
// thing at the declaration.
//
// The candidate list comes from `SemanticCheck.overloadCandidates` — the ONE staleness filter over
// `overloadSets` — so a member left behind by a previous fold is inert here exactly as it is at a call.
// An empty list is a requirement the type declares nothing for: the set holds only names this project no
// longer registers, which is the MISSING case and not a mismatch.
function checkOverloadedMembers(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, rootInterfaceName ByteArray, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures, members ByteArrayArray, missing StringArray, wrongSignature StringArray)
	let candidates = overloadCandidates(project, members: members)
	if candidates.count() == 0 'noMemberSurvives'
		pushMissingRequirement(missing, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, rootInterfaceName: rootInterfaceName)
		return
	end 'noMemberSurvives'

	var matched = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for candidate in candidates 'eachCandidate'
		if signatureMatches(project, method: collectedMethod.method, conformingType: conformance.typeName, implFunc: conformanceImplFunction(functionsByName, name: candidate), implSig: conformanceImplSignature(project, name: candidate), typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures) 'matches'
			matched.push(candidate)
		end 'matches'
	end 'eachCandidate'

	if matched.count() != 1 'notExactlyOne'
		wrongSignature.push(overloadMismatchLine(project, conformance: conformance, method: collectedMethod.method, functionsByName: functionsByName, candidates: candidates, matched: matched, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures))
		return
	end 'notExactlyOne'

	let selected = try matched.get(0) otherwise panic("checkOverloadedMembers: matched.count() is 1 but get(0) failed")
	acceptConformingMember(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, selected: selected, implFunc: conformanceImplFunction(functionsByName, name: selected), typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)
end 'checkOverloadedMembers'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE PLACE A REQUIREMENT IS DECLARED SATISFIED — and the place the WITNESS TABLE learns by which
// member (R10).** The selection is filed under the slot's own key so `ensureWitnessTable` stamps the
// relocation from THIS answer rather than re-joining `<Type>.<method>` for itself; see `WitnessSlotImplMap`
// for the silent wrong dispatch that second derivation was.
//
// The THROWS relation is checked only once the structural signature agrees, mirroring the oracle's
// `else if (method.ThrowsTypeName != null)` branch: on a shape mismatch the throws question is noise. It is
// asked of the SELECTED member's function, which under an overload set is not necessarily the bare-named one.
function acceptConformingMember(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, selected ByteArray, implFunc IrFunction, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	recordWitnessSlotImpl(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, selected: selected, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)
	validateThrowsConformance(project, signatures: signatures, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, implFunc: implFunc)
end 'acceptConformingMember'

// File the accepted member under its witness slot's key (R10). An entry already present must AGREE: two
// conformances of one type routinely reach the same `(conformer, interface, method)` slot — `implements Sub,
// Base` where `Sub extends Base` collects Base's requirements twice — and they normally select identically,
// because the selection is a function of the conformer and the requirement's SUBSTITUTED signature alone.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A DISAGREEMENT IS THE AUTHOR'S, NOT THE COMPILER'S, AND IT PANICKED UNTIL THE R10 REVIEW.** The
// substituted signature is exactly what an associated-type binding varies, so two routes to one slot that
// bind that interface's `uses` names differently compare DIFFERENT requirements under one key and can select
// different members. Both spellings are ordinary source and both were rejected cleanly as E3016 before R10
// (the bare key could satisfy only one of the two requirements, so the other reported a wrong signature) —
// matching a requirement against the whole overload set is what lets both routes SUCCEED and disagree:
//
//   • `implements Conv with Whole, Conv with Real` — one interface named twice, bound two ways.
//   • `implements Child with Whole, Parent with Real`, `interface Child extends Parent uses Item` — reaches
//     `Parent` twice while naming no interface twice, so no rule about the SHAPE of an `implements` clause
//     could have caught it. This is why the condition is detected where the two selections MEET.
//
// ⚠ **THE SLOT IS THE UNIT OF THE CONTRADICTION, so the diagnostic is filed against the slot rather than
// against the clause.** One witness table per (conformer, interface) with one address per method slot is the
// dictionary's shape, so an interface has to be conformed to exactly one way; there is nothing to choose by
// and nothing the lowering could do with two answers but flip a coin.
function recordWitnessSlotImpl(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, selected ByteArray, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	let key = witnessSlotImplKey(conformance.typeName, interfaceName: collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName, methodName: collectedMethod.method.name)
	if let existing = try project.witnessSlotImpls.get(key) 'alreadySelected'
		if not existing.equals(selected) 'disagrees'
			reportConflictingConformance(project, functionsByName: functionsByName, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, existing: existing, selected: selected, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)
		end 'disagrees'
		return
	end 'alreadySelected'

	project.witnessSlotImpls.upsert(key, value: selected)
end 'recordWitnessSlotImpl'

// E3111's sentence, anchored on the conforming type's NAME token like every other conformance diagnostic in
// this file. The two members are rendered through the SAME `renderMemberSignatures` an overload mismatch
// prints, for its reason: a registration name (`convert#Real`) is a key this compiler mints, and telling an
// author to go and look at it names something their source does not contain.
function reportConflictingConformance(project Project, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, existing ByteArray, selected ByteArray, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	var conflicting = ByteArrayArray.create()
	conflicting.push(existing)
	conflicting.push(selected)

	let interfaceName = String.from(collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName)
	let rendered = renderMemberSignatures(project, conformance: conformance, method: collectedMethod.method, functionsByName: functionsByName, members: conflicting, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticConflictingInterfaceConformance, message: "Type '{String.from(conformance.typeName)}' reaches interface '{interfaceName}''s requirement '{String.from(collectedMethod.method.name)}' by two routes that select different members: {rendered}. A conforming type has ONE witness table per interface and ONE address per method slot, so '{interfaceName}' must be conformed to exactly one way — bind its associated types once, either by removing the duplicate '{interfaceName}' entry or by not also reaching it through an interface that '{String.from(conformance.typeName)}' already implements", line: conformance.nameLine, column: conformance.nameColumn, filePath: conformance.filePath)
end 'reportConflictingConformance'

// The MISSING-method line for a requirement the conforming type declares nothing under, tagged
// `(from Parent)` when the requirement was inherited rather than the root interface's own. One home because
// two arms reach it — a name in no registry at all, and an overload set none of whose members this project
// still registers.
function pushMissingRequirement(missing StringArray, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, rootInterfaceName ByteArray)
	let expected = formatInterfaceMethodSignature(collectedMethod.method)
	if collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName.equals(rootInterfaceName) 'ownMethod'
		missing.push(expected)
		return
	end 'ownMethod'

	missing.push("{expected} (from {String.from(collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName)})")
end 'pushMissingRequirement'

// The merged module's function for a member of an overload set. Every registration is followed by a module
// function AND a `funcSignatures` entry in the same `parseFunction`, and `overloadCandidates` has already
// dropped anything absent from the latter — so a member missing from the module is a merge-invariant
// violation, not a conformance miss.
//
// ⚠ **WHY THE IMPL SIDE IS READ OFF THE MODULE AND NOT OFF `funcSignatures`, WHICH THIS CHECK ALSO HOLDS.**
// The module function's `maxonParamTypes` / `maxonReturnType` are the REMAPPED (project-interner) types,
// while `funcSignatures`' param TYPES are deliberately left file-local until `resolveTypes` overwrites them
// (`ParseStaging.remapArtifact`) and so are not safe to render across files. Param NAMES go the other way:
// they are interner-free `ByteArray`s and are the one thing the module's `IrFunction` does not carry, which
// is why `conformanceImplSignature` below exists beside this and neither one subsumes the other.
function conformanceImplFunction(functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, name ByteArray) returns IrFunction
	return try functionsByName.funcNamed(name) otherwise panic("conformanceImplFunction: overload-set member '{String.from(name)}' is in funcSignatures but the merged module declares no function under that name")
end 'conformanceImplFunction'

// Every method records BOTH a module function and a `funcSignatures` entry (parseFunction), so a function
// present in the module without a signature is a merge-invariant violation, not a miss.
function conformanceImplSignature(project Project, name ByteArray) returns FuncSignature
	return try project.funcSignatures.get(name) otherwise panic("conformanceImplSignature: '{String.from(name)}' is in the module but has no funcSignatures entry")
end 'conformanceImplSignature'

// ⭐ **THE WRONG-SIGNATURE LINE FOR AN OVERLOADED NAME MUST NOT SPEAK AS THOUGH ONE CANDIDATE EXISTED
// (R10).** E3016's line is `<actual> (expected <required>)`, which is exactly right for a name declared
// once — and a lie for a set of two, where naming whichever member held the bare key told the author to
// change a method that may be perfectly correct. Both arms therefore say HOW MANY members carry the name and
// render all of the ones that matter: every candidate when none matches, and only the tied ones when several
// do (the others are irrelevant to an ambiguity).
function overloadMismatchLine(project Project, conformance ConformanceDecl, method InterfaceMethodSig, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, candidates ByteArrayArray, matched ByteArrayArray, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns String
	let expected = formatInterfaceMethodSignature(method)
	if matched.count() == 0 'noneMatches'
		return "no member named '{String.from(method.name)}' matches: {renderMemberSignatures(project, conformance: conformance, method: method, functionsByName: functionsByName, members: candidates, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)} (expected {expected})"
	end 'noneMatches'

	return "{matched.count()} members named '{String.from(method.name)}' match: {renderMemberSignatures(project, conformance: conformance, method: method, functionsByName: functionsByName, members: matched, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)} (expected {expected})"
end 'overloadMismatchLine'

// `members`' signatures as one comma-separated source string, each through the SAME `formatActualSignature`
// a single member's mismatch prints — so a set's line and a lone member's line cannot drift in format. Each
// is rendered under the REQUIREMENT's method name rather than its own registration name: `label#Code` is a
// key, not something an author wrote.
//
// The join is `Parser.appendCommaSeparated`, the ONE `", "` a diagnostic's list is punctuated with — a
// second accumulator here is how two messages listing the same kind of thing come to look different.
function renderMemberSignatures(project Project, conformance ConformanceDecl, method InterfaceMethodSig, functionsByName FunctionNameIndex, members ByteArrayArray, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns String
	var rendered = ""
	for member in members 'each'
		rendered = appendCommaSeparated(rendered, item: formatActualSignature(project, methodName: method.name, conformingType: conformance.typeName, implFunc: conformanceImplFunction(functionsByName, name: member), implSig: conformanceImplSignature(project, name: member), typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures))
	end 'each'
	return rendered
end 'renderMemberSignatures'

// ⭐⭐ **THE THROWS RELATION BETWEEN AN INTERFACE METHOD AND ITS IMPL, CHECKED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS (P1.7a
// slice 2b-v).** A witness dispatch picks its ABI from the INTERFACE — a `throws` method is dispatched as
// the dual-register `witnessTryCall`, a non-throwing one as the single-register `witnessCall` — while the
// callee returns through the ABI its OWN declaration chose. The two must agree, and only this check can
// make them:
//
//   • **impl does not throw where the interface does** — the reference oracles' rule, wording and all
//     (`ValidateThrowsConformance` / v1's `validateThrowsConformance`). The `try` at the dispatch would
//     branch on an error-flag register the callee never wrote, i.e. on whatever the ABI left in R10.
//   • **impl throws where the interface does not** — ⚠ **shv2's OWN rule; NEITHER reference has it**, and
//     the divergence is deliberate. Both oracles check the relation only when the interface method declares
//     `throws`, so an impl that throws under a non-throwing interface method is accepted by both. In shv2
//     that program dispatches through the single-register `witnessCall` — the caller reads the primary
//     register and nothing reads the flag — so a thrown error is SILENTLY DISCARDED and the impl's
//     throw-path primary comes back as a real answer. That is precisely the defect this slice exists to fix,
//     arriving from the other side, and it is not diagnosable anywhere else: at the dispatch the compiler
//     sees only the interface, and at the impl it sees only the type.
//
//   • **the two thrown TYPES differ, UNLESS THE REQUIREMENT IS ABSTRACT** — ⚠ **shv2's OWN rule too;
//     NEITHER reference has it** (the bootstrap's
//     `ValidateThrowsConformance` asks only that the impl throws SOMETHING conforming to `Error`), and it is
//     the same divergence for the third time. Under MONOMORPHIZATION, which is what both oracles do, a `try`
//     is typed off the CALLEE's own clause, so a differing impl type is harmless — the caught error is
//     whatever the impl actually throws. Under shv2's DICTIONARY-PASSING there is no callee to ask: the `try`
//     at the dispatch types its `(e)` binding off the INTERFACE, so an impl throwing a different enum has its
//     ordinals decoded as the interface's. MEASURED, both halves: an impl throwing `OtherError.one` under an
//     interface declaring `throws DigestError` was caught as `DigestError.beta` (a silent wrong answer), and a
//     SCALAR-throwing impl under a BOXED-union interface clause made the `try` treat the ordinal as a box
//     pointer and SEGFAULT. `checkPropagateType`'s E3059 cannot see it — that check compares the INTERFACE's
//     clause against the enclosing function's, and those two agree in exactly this program.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE THIRD RULE HAS ONE EXEMPTION, AND IT IS THE ONE `Error` EXISTS FOR (A1s).** `stdlib`'s own
// `interface Parsable` declares `static function fromString(input String) returns Self throws Error`, and
// every conformer in the corpus throws its own concrete error enum — an impl being MORE SPECIFIC than the
// requirement. The rule above is stated as "the same NAME", but the fact it protects is "the same DECODE":
// the `try` at the dispatch types its `(e)` binding off the requirement, so the impl's flag must be exactly
// what that binding reads back. For a requirement naming a concrete enum, same-decode IS same-name — one
// enum's ordinals are not another's. For a requirement naming an INTERFACE it is not: a marker interface
// declares no case, `match e` over it is E2015, and the binding is an opaque scalar with no ordinal to get
// wrong. `throwsRequirementIsAbstract` is where same-name stops standing in for same-decode.
//
// ⚠ **THE FLAG SHAPE STILL HAS TO AGREE, so the exemption reaches only a SCALAR impl error**
// (`requireImplErrorIsANarrowableError`). An abstract requirement is caught through the `ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`
// ABI — `Parser.throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion` answers `false` for a name in no enum registry — while a
// payload-carrying union hands its error over as a heap BOX POINTER. MEASURED on the plain direct-call
// spelling of that pair (`function f() returns Code throws Error` throwing a boxed union): **exit 101, a
// leaked box, in shv2 AND in the C# oracle.** This rung refused the witness-dispatch route into it rather
// than opening a second door to it; the FIRST door was shut by A1s-throwsbox, whose E3113
// (`SemanticCheck.checkThrowsClausesNameDeclaredErrors`) requires a FUNCTION's own clause to name a declared
// enum or union. The two checks own disjoint sides — this one the relation between a requirement and its
// impl, that one a function's own clause — so an impl method reaches both, and reaches THIS one first
// (`checkConformance` runs before the pipeline) because only it can name the requirement being violated.
//
// Split on the INTERFACE's clause first: each side then asks a different question of the impl's, and both
// answers come out of a `match` on it rather than a boolean pair, so a clause is never re-extracted behind an
// `otherwise panic` that has to argue it cannot fire.
//
// ⚠ **THE AGREEING PATH ALLOCATES NOTHING, AND THAT IS WHY THE DECISION REPORTS RATHER THAN RETURNS.** The
// obvious shape here is a `ThrowsConformanceVerdict{agree, violated(message)}` returned up to one report site.
// It was written that way and MEASURED: the `agree` box is 16 bytes, paid once per CONFORMING METHOD of every
// program that declares any conformance, and scale-test read it straight off — +16 / +24 / +40 / +72 / +136 /
// +264 allocations across the six rungs (exactly 16 bytes each), landing OUTSIDE every per-phase bucket
// because `checkConformance` runs before the pipeline. Passing `project` down and reporting in the arm that
// decides costs one extra parameter and allocates only when a diagnostic is actually produced.
function validateThrowsConformance(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, implFunc IrFunction)
	match collectedMethod.method.throwsClause 'iface'
		none then requireImplDoesNotThrow(project, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, implClause: implFunc.throwsTypeName)
		throwing(required) then requireImplThrowsSameType(project, signatures: signatures, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, required: required, implClause: implFunc.throwsTypeName)
	end 'iface'
end 'validateThrowsConformance'

// The interface method declares NO throws clause: the impl may not declare one either (shv2's own rule).
function requireImplDoesNotThrow(project Project, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, implClause ThrowsClause)
	match implClause 'impl'
		none then break 'impl'
		throwing(declared) then reportThrowsViolation(project, conformance: conformance, message: "{throwsConformanceSubject(conformance.typeName, collectedMethod: collectedMethod)} throws '{declared}' but interface '{String.from(collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName)}' declares it non-throwing — a witness dispatch of a non-throwing interface method reads no error flag, so the error would be silently dropped")
	end 'impl'
end 'requireImplDoesNotThrow'

// The interface method declares `throws required`: the impl must declare a throws clause, and it must name
// the SAME error type — the dispatch site types its caught error off `required`, whatever the impl throws.
function requireImplThrowsSameType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, required String, implClause ThrowsClause)
	match implClause 'impl'
		// The oracles' wording verbatim.
		none then reportThrowsViolation(project, conformance: conformance, message: "{throwsConformanceSubject(conformance.typeName, collectedMethod: collectedMethod)} must throw '{required}' as required by interface '{String.from(collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName)}'")
		throwing(declared) then requireSameErrorType(project, signatures: signatures, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, required: required, declared: declared)
	end 'impl'
end 'requireImplThrowsSameType'

// Both sides throw: they must name the same error type — unless the REQUIREMENT is ABSTRACT, which is the
// one narrowing the header above exempts (A1s). Split out because the comparison is a labelled `if`, which a
// `match` arm cannot hold inline (the shape `rejectIfDifferentThrows` is split for).
//
// ⚠ **THE EXEMPTION IS ASKED ONLY AFTER THE NAMES DISAGREE, and that placement is the whole of its cost.**
// An agreeing pair — every conformance in the corpus that does not narrow, and every one in the scale ladder
// — returns above without asking either question, so this rung adds no allocation to any program that
// compiled before it. Both questions below box a union (`InterfaceLookup`, `DeclaredEnum`), which the header
// of `validateThrowsConformance` records scale-test reading straight off a per-conforming-method box; here
// they are paid only where the old rule was about to produce a diagnostic.
function requireSameErrorType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, required String, declared String)
	if declared.equals(required) 'sameErrorType'
		return
	end 'sameErrorType'

	if throwsRequirementIsAbstract(project, signatures: signatures, required: required) 'abstractRequirement'
		requireImplErrorIsANarrowableError(project, signatures: signatures, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, required: required, declared: declared)
		return
	end 'abstractRequirement'

	reportThrowsViolation(project, conformance: conformance, message: "{throwsConformanceSubject(conformance.typeName, collectedMethod: collectedMethod)} throws '{declared}' but interface '{String.from(collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName)}' declares it 'throws {required}' — a witness dispatch types its caught error off the INTERFACE, so the impl's error would be decoded as '{required}'")
end 'requireSameErrorType'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THE REQUIREMENT'S `throws` TYPE NAME AN INTERFACE RATHER THAN A CONCRETE ERROR — i.e. is there
// anything for the dispatch's `(e)` binding to decode WRONG (A1s)?** The sorting is
// `classifyThrowsClauseName`'s, below, where the reason each of its three answers exists is written down
// ONCE; what belongs here is only what this caller does with them. `undeclared` reads as NOT abstract, which
// is the whole point of the classifier's third box: a typo'd requirement must not buy the abstract
// requirement's narrowing, so it falls to the strict same-name rule and is refused by it.
function throwsRequirementIsAbstract(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, required String) returns bool
	// `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath`: a requirement's `throws` type is written inside an `interface`, which
	// records no declaring file — see the classifier's own header.
	return match classifyThrowsClauseName(project, signatures: signatures, name: required, readerFilePath: CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath) 'kind'
		concreteError gives false
		abstractInterface gives true
		undeclared gives false
	end 'kind'
end 'throwsRequirementIsAbstract'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A `throws` CLAUSE NAME DENOTES — ONE CLASSIFIER, FOR THE TWO RULES THAT ASK (A1s-throwsbox
// review).** The two questions above and `SemanticCheck.requireThrowsClauseNamesADeclaredError`'s E3113 are
// about DIFFERENT things — this file owns the relation between a requirement's clause and its impl's, that
// one owns a function's own clause — but both begin by sorting one name into the SAME three boxes, and both
// were sorting it with their own copy of the two lookups. Nothing made the copies agree, and the way they
// would have come apart is silent: teach one of them that a `typealias` to an enum is concrete too, or that
// a user's own `enum Error` is special, and the impl side and the plain-function side would disagree about
// what an error type IS, with no compile error anywhere to say so. This project's signature bug, in the exact
// place A1s-throwsbox was written to remove one instance of it.
//
// The three boxes are exhaustive by construction, and each of the two conditions rules out a wrong answer the
// other cannot:
//
//   • **`concreteError` — it names a declared enum/union**, asked of the registry the catch DECODE itself
//     consults (`Parser.throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion` → `enumInAnyFile(name)`), so it is the only honest answer to "can
//     this be discriminated?". Asking that rather than testing the literal name `Error` is what keeps a
//     user's own `enum Error` — which the language accepts — from being read as abstract: for that program
//     the clause IS concrete. `containsEnum` and not `enumOf`, because no caller here needs a layout and
//     `enumOf` would box one to throw it away.
//   • **`abstractInterface` — it names an INTERFACE.** "Decodes nothing" alone would have made a TYPO
//     (`throws Bogus`, a name in no registry at all) indistinguishable from a deliberate abstract channel,
//     and the author's mistake would read as a licence. Abstractness is something a clause DECLARES, not
//     something it fails to be. `lookupInterface` answers for the synthesized `Error` and for a user
//     interface alike, off the one door every other conformance question in this file already asks through.
//   • **`undeclared` — neither**, which is a THIRD answer and not a shading of the second: a struct, a
//     ranged alias, a generic type parameter, a stdlib type shv2 does not declare, or a typo.
// The three — and only three — things a `throws` clause name can denote. See `classifyThrowsClauseName`.
export enum ThrowsClauseKind
	// A declared enum or union: the catch decode can discriminate its cases.
	concreteError
	// An interface: an abstract error channel, declaring no case to decode.
	abstractInterface
	// Neither — a struct, a ranged alias, a type parameter, a type shv2 does not declare, or a typo.
	undeclared
end 'ThrowsClauseKind'

// ⚠ **IT SORTS THE NAME AS `readerFilePath` MEANS IT, NOT AS THE PROGRAM DOES.** A `throws` clause names a
// type in a FILE, and both boxes it can land in are registries a cross-KIND contest can give to another
// declaration — so a `throws Fault` written where `Fault` means a ranged alias would otherwise have been
// sorted `concreteError` off a DIFFERENT file's enum, and the catch would decode against cases the clause's
// own file never named. The two callers each have a reader to give: a plain function's is its declaring
// file, and an interface REQUIREMENT's is `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath` — a requirement is written inside an
// `interface`, which records no declaring file (`SemanticCheck.witnessFormalDeclaredType` says the same).
export function classifyThrowsClauseName(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, name String, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ThrowsClauseKind
	let key = name.toByteArray()
	if signatures.containsEnumFor(key, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'namesAConcreteError'
		return ThrowsClauseKind.concreteError
	end 'namesAConcreteError'

	return match lookupInterface(project.interfaces, name: key) 'iface'
		found gives ThrowsClauseKind.abstractInterface if not signatures.aliasShadowsNominalFor(key, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) else ThrowsClauseKind.undeclared
		notFound gives ThrowsClauseKind.undeclared
	end 'iface'
end 'classifyThrowsClauseName'

// The impl's error must reach the dispatch through the ABI an abstract requirement is CAUGHT with — the
// scalar `ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias` flag. A payload-carrying union's flag is the heap box pointer
// itself (`Parser.emitThrowError` stamps `errorIsBoxed` off the thrown value and `lowerThrowError` writes the
// pointer verbatim), so under an abstract requirement the handler would subtract the bias from a pointer and
// never release the box. Read through the SAME `enumInAnyFile(name).isBoxed()` the catch decode reads, for that
// function's own reason: a boxed union must not be encoded as a pointer at one end and decoded as an ordinal
// at the other, and two spellings of "is it boxed?" is exactly how that comes to be true.
//
// ⚠⚠ **"NOT FOUND IN THE ENUM REGISTRY" IS NOT EVIDENCE THE FLAG IS SCALAR — IT IS THE COMPILER HAVING NO
// ANSWER, AND IT GETS ITS OWN REFUSAL (A1s review).** As first written this asked `enumInAnyFile(declared)` and
// read the `undeclared` arm as `false`, i.e. as "not boxed, therefore fine" — the PERMISSIVE reading of a
// memory-safety question, taken for a name the compiler could not resolve. MEASURED on the shipped rung:
// an impl declaring `throws Bogus`, a name in no registry at all, was ACCEPTED under `throws Error` and
// linked and ran, where the strict same-name rule this exemption relaxes had refused it.
//
// It is the same hazard `throwsRequirementIsAbstract`'s SECOND conjunct exists for, one side over: there
// the argument is that abstractness is something a requirement DECLARES rather than something it fails to
// be, so a typo must not read as a licence. The impl side owes the identical argument — a resolvable error
// type is something an implementation DECLARES — and did not make it. Both arms below therefore report;
// neither is silent, which is what CLAUDE.md's "no silent unhandled cases" asks of a `match` like this one.
//
// ⚠ It refuses only pairs the OLD rule already refused: a same-named pair returns from `requireSameErrorType`
// before either question is asked, so nothing that compiled before A1s can reach here. (`throws Bogus` on a
// PLAIN function was accepted when this was written; A1s-throwsbox's E3113 closed that gap, and this arm
// still owns the IMPL-side spelling because it alone can name the requirement the impl is violating.)
function requireImplErrorIsANarrowableError(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, required String, declared String)
	match signatures.enumInAnyFile(declared.toByteArray()) 'implError'
		declared(layout) then requireImplErrorConformsAndIsScalar(project, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, required: required, declared: declared, layout: layout)
		undeclared then reportThrowsViolation(project, conformance: conformance, message: "{throwsConformanceSubject(conformance.typeName, collectedMethod: collectedMethod)} throws '{declared}' but interface '{String.from(collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName)}' declares it 'throws {required}', and '{declared}' names no declared enum or union — the narrowing an abstract requirement permits is granted only to an error type whose flag SHAPE the compiler can see, and an unresolvable name is a mistake rather than a licence. Declare '{declared}', or name '{required}' itself")
	end 'implError'
end 'requireImplErrorIsANarrowableError'

// ⭐⭐ **AN ABSTRACT REQUIREMENT NARROWS TO A CONFORMER, NOT TO ANY ENUM AT ALL (A1s wave 2).** The narrowing
// an abstract `throws Error` permits is "an error type", and this is the half that says WHICH: the impl's
// enum must DECLARE `implements <the requirement's interface>`. Without it the exemption read `throws Error`
// as `throws anything`, a strictly weaker rule than the same-name one it replaced.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED AGAINST THE ORACLE, NOT ARGUED**: `enum NotAnError { Bad = 1 }`, declaring no conformance,
// used as a `Parsable.fromString`'s `throws` type is refused by the bootstrap with **`error E3016: … Method
// 'Value.fromString' throws 'NotAnError' which does not conform to Error`**, and was ACCEPTED here — it
// compiled, linked and ran. `specs-shv2/parsable-interface.md`'s `error.throws-non-error-type` is the pin,
// and it names why the gap shipped with wave 1: the case that catches it could not be enabled until the
// stdlib module declaring `interface Parsable` was loadable.
//
// ⚠ **THE ANSWER COMES OFF THE ENUM'S OWN LAYOUT, NOT OFF `project.conformances`.** A `ConformanceDecl` is
// recorded only by `Parser.parseTypeDeclaration`; an `enum`/`union` `implements` clause is a different door
// (`readOptionalImplementsClause`) and files its names on the layout instead, because an enum has no METHODS
// for `checkConformance` to check against a requirement. Asking `project.conformances` here answers `false`
// for EVERY error enum in the corpus — measured, on the first cut of this check: all five `Parsable`
// conformers were refused, each naming its own `implements Error`.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS ASKED AFTER RESOLVABILITY, WHICH IS THE DIAGNOSTIC AND NOT AN ORDERING PREFERENCE.** A name
// in no registry is refused by the arm above with the message that says exactly that; telling that author
// their type "does not conform to Error" would blame the wrong thing about a type that does not exist.
// `error.throws-unresolvable-impl-type-under-abstract-requirement` is the pin, and it caught this order
// being wrong.
//
// The wording is the oracles' verbatim, exactly as `requireImplThrowsSameType`'s missing-clause arm is.
function requireImplErrorConformsAndIsScalar(project Project, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, required String, declared String, layout EnumLayout)
	if not conformanceListDeclares(layout.conformsTo, interfaceName: required.toByteArray()) 'doesNotConform'
		reportThrowsViolation(project, conformance: conformance, message: "{throwsConformanceSubject(conformance.typeName, collectedMethod: collectedMethod)} throws '{declared}' which does not conform to {required}")
		return
	end 'doesNotConform'

	refuseBoxedImplError(project, conformance: conformance, collectedMethod: collectedMethod, required: required, declared: declared, implIsBoxed: layout.isBoxed())
end 'requireImplErrorConformsAndIsScalar'

// The boxed half of the question above, split out because the verdict is a labelled `if` — the shape a
// `match` arm cannot hold inline, and the same reason `requireSameErrorType` is split out of
// `requireImplThrowsSameType`. Takes the ANSWER rather than the layout so the payload binding stays in the
// one arm that produced it.
function refuseBoxedImplError(project Project, conformance ConformanceDecl, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod, required String, declared String, implIsBoxed bool)
	if not implIsBoxed 'scalarFlag'
		return
	end 'scalarFlag'

	reportThrowsViolation(project, conformance: conformance, message: "{throwsConformanceSubject(conformance.typeName, collectedMethod: collectedMethod)} throws '{declared}' but interface '{String.from(collectedMethod.sourceInterfaceName)}' declares it 'throws {required}', which declares no case to decode — a witness dispatch catches such a requirement through the SCALAR error-flag ABI, while a payload-carrying union is handed over as a heap box pointer that would be decoded as an ordinal and never released. Throw a payload-free enum, or declare the requirement as '{declared}' itself")
end 'refuseBoxedImplError'

// The ONE report site for every throws-conformance violation. Not a pass-through: it derives four of
// `reportPositioned`'s six arguments from `conformance` (the code, and the position the `implements` clause
// anchors on), which each arm would otherwise spell out separately — and it has already earned that once:
// A1s added a FOURTH violation (a boxed impl error under an abstract requirement) and spelled none of them.
function reportThrowsViolation(project Project, conformance ConformanceDecl, message String)
	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticPartialInterfaceImpl, message: message, line: conformance.nameLine, column: conformance.nameColumn, filePath: conformance.filePath)
end 'reportThrowsViolation'

// `Method 'Type.method'` — the subject all three throws-conformance diagnostics open with, spelled once.
function throwsConformanceSubject(typeName ByteArray, collectedMethod CollectedInterfaceMethod) returns String
	return "Method '{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(collectedMethod.method.name)}'"
end 'throwsConformanceSubject'

// Does the impl method's signature match the interface method's? Instance methods carry `__self` at
// position 0, dropped on both sides; a `Self` in the interface substitutes the conforming type. The two
// sides are compared as the SOURCE SPELLS them, because a `typealias` names its own type: `process(v Int)`
// does not implement `process(v Integer)` even where the two ranges are identical, and the diagnostic
// therefore quotes exactly what it compared.
//
// ⚠ **`method` IS ALREADY ASSOCIATED-TYPE-SUBSTITUTED and this function must not do it again** — see
// `collectInterfaceMethods`. What it still substitutes is `Self`, and that ASYMMETRY WITH THE MESSAGE IS
// DELIBERATE AND PINNED: a `Self` requirement compares against the conforming type but PRINTS as `Self`
// (`specs-shv2/comparable.md` pins `- compare(other Self) returns Ordering` and
// `(expected compare(other Self) returns Ordering)`), which is the bootstrap's split too — its comparison
// maps the interface name through `ResolveInterfaceTypeName` while its message formatter is handed only the
// associated-type dict. `Self` reads better in a diagnostic than the reader's own type name repeated back;
// an associated type does not, because its name is meaningless outside the interface that declared it.
function signatureMatches(project Project, method InterfaceMethodSig, conformingType ByteArray, implFunc IrFunction, implSig FuncSignature, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns bool
	// ⭐ **A `static` REQUIREMENT IS MET BY A STATIC MEMBER, AND AN INSTANCE ONE BY AN INSTANCE MEMBER (R9).**
	// The two have different ABIs — an instance method takes `__self` at position 0 — and the witness slot is
	// stamped with whichever the type declared, so an instance impl under a static requirement would be
	// dispatched with no receiver in the register it reads `self` from. Neither reference has this rule: both
	// skip a present static outright, so neither can see the disagreement. `takesReceiver()` (does the
	// signature declare `__self`?) is exactly "is an instance method", so it is also what the parameter
	// comparison below indexes by — one fact, asked once.
	let implIsStatic = not implSig.takesReceiver()
	if method.isStatic != implIsStatic 'receiverKindDiffers'
		return false
	end 'receiverKindDiffers'

	let selfOffset = 0 if implIsStatic else 1
	if method.paramNames.count() != implFunc.maxonParamTypes.count() - selfOffset 'arityDiffers'
		return false
	end 'arityDiffers'

	for i in 0 upto method.paramNames.count() 'eachParam'
		let implType = try implFunc.maxonParamTypes.get(i + selfOffset) otherwise panic("signatureMatches: maxonParamTypes.get OOB at {i + selfOffset}")

		// ⭐⭐ **A REQUIREMENT SPELLED BARE `__ManagedMemory` MEANS *ANY* BUFFER, NOT A BUFFER OF BYTES.**
		// In the reference the buffer is GENERIC over one `Element`, so the bare spelling inside a
		// requirement is that unbound parameter. shv2 resolves it to the CONCRETE byte instance, so by the
		// time `paramTypeNames` is rendered it reads `Array_Byte` and an impl declaring
		// `__ManagedMemory with Key` is refused against the very interface written for it —
		// `BuiltinDictionaryLiteral` against `stdlib/Map.maxon`.
		//
		// ⛔ **THIS MAY NOT BE DONE BY COMPARING MORE LOOSELY.** Widening every `Array_Byte` requirement
		// would silently accept a genuine `ByteArray` mismatch — a wrong ACCEPTANCE, which nothing reports.
		// The gate is `paramIsBuffer`, the SPELLING carried from `parseInterfaceMethod`'s tokens, so the
		// widening reaches exactly the positions that meant "any element" and no others.
		//
		// The impl side must still BE a buffer instance: any `Array with T` qualifies (in shv2 a buffer and
		// an `Array` are one record — the spelling picks the member surface, not the layout), and anything
		// else — a struct, a primitive, a `String` — does not.
		let requirementTakesAnyBuffer = try method.paramIsBuffer.get(i) otherwise false
		if requirementTakesAnyBuffer 'requirementTakesAnyBuffer'
			if not signatures.isArrayInstanceAt(maxonTypeTag(implType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(implType)) 'implIsNotABuffer'
				return false
			end 'implIsNotABuffer'
			continue
		end 'requirementTakesAnyBuffer'

		let expected = substituteSelf(try method.paramTypeNames.get(i) otherwise panic("signatureMatches: paramTypeNames.get OOB at {i}"), conformingType: conformingType)
		let actual = renderDeclaredTypeName(implType, declarationName: conformingType, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, typeNames: project.typeNames, signatures: signatures)
		if expected.equals(actual) or aliasDecaysIntoPrimitive(project, expected: expected, actual: actual) 'oneSpelling'
			continue
		end 'oneSpelling'

		// A requirement that spelled a generic-instance ALIAS renders as that alias (`Parser.renderInterfaceParamTypes`),
		// which no impl rendering equals: the two are compared as an identity through the alias registry AND a
		// brand against the impl's spelled column, exactly as `aggregatesConflict` compares a value — an
		// unbranded side fits any brand, two different aliases of one instance do not.
		if maxonTypeTag(implType) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'aBrandedInstance'
			if not signatures.instanceIdentityOfSpelling(expected).equals(signatures.canonicalInstanceName(genericInstanceIdOf(implType))) 'differentInstances'
				return false
			end 'differentInstances'

			if brandsConflict(signatures.instanceBrandOfSpelling(expected), got: implParamSpelling(implSig, position: i + selfOffset)) 'twoBrandsOfOneInstance'
				return false
			end 'twoBrandsOfOneInstance'

			continue
		end 'aBrandedInstance'

		return false
	end 'eachParam'

	let expectedReturn = substituteSelf(method.returnTypeName, conformingType: conformingType)
	let actualReturn = renderReturnType(project, returnType: implFunc.maxonReturnType, conformingType: conformingType, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures)
	return expectedReturn.equals(actualReturn) or aliasDecaysIntoPrimitive(project, expected: expectedReturn, actual: actualReturn)
end 'signatureMatches'

// ⭐ **A REQUIREMENT WRITTEN AS A BARE PRIMITIVE IS AN UNNAMED SLOT, AND AN ALIAS OVER THAT PRIMITIVE
// FITS IT — AT A PARAMETER AND AT THE RETURN.** `take(e float)` — an associated type bound to `float` — is
// implemented by `take(e Real)`, and `give() returns float` by `give() returns Real`: an unnamed value
// decays into any alias slot of its class and a named value into an unnamed one
// (`ProgramSignatures.aggregatesConflict`). The reverse — a requirement spelling an alias, an impl spelling
// the primitive or another alias — is the nominal refusal `signatureMatches` makes.
function aliasDecaysIntoPrimitive(project Project, expected ByteArray, actual ByteArray) returns bool
	let underlying = try project.typeAliases.underlyingInAnyFile(actual) otherwise return false
	return maxonTypeName(underlying, typeNames: project.typeNames).toByteArray().equals(expected)
end 'aliasDecaysIntoPrimitive'

// The impl method's signature as a source string — `process(value Float) returns Integer`. Param NAMES
// come from `funcSignatures` (interner-free); param TYPES from the module's REMAPPED `maxonParamTypes`.
// Both are rendered to the same source-string columns the interface stores, then handed to the ONE
// signature assembler — so an actual and an expected signature cannot drift in format.
function formatActualSignature(project Project, methodName ByteArray, conformingType ByteArray, implFunc IrFunction, implSig FuncSignature, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns String
	let implIsStatic = not implSig.takesReceiver()
	let selfOffset = 0 if implIsStatic else 1
	var paramNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
	var paramTypes = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for i in selfOffset upto implFunc.maxonParamTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		paramNames.push(try implSig.paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("formatActualSignature: paramNames.get OOB at {i}"))
		paramTypes.push(implParamDisplay(project, implFunc: implFunc, implSig: implSig, position: i, conformingType: conformingType, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures))
	end 'eachParam'
	return assembleMethodSignature(methodName, paramNames: paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, returnType: renderReturnType(project, returnType: implFunc.maxonReturnType, conformingType: conformingType, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, signatures: signatures), isStatic: implIsStatic)
end 'formatActualSignature'

// An interface method's signature as a source string — `process(value Integer) returns Integer` — from
// its stored (already source-rendered, already associated-type-substituted) columns, through the same
// assembler the impl side uses. It performs NO substitution of its own, which is the whole point: the
// sentence and the verdict read one string (`collectInterfaceMethods`).
// The alias an impl parameter was DECLARED under — the signature's spelled column for a generic-instance
// parameter (`Parser.paramDeclaredNamesOf`), empty where it was spelled inline.
function implParamSpelling(implSig FuncSignature, position ParamCount) returns ByteArray
	return try implSig.paramDeclaredNames.get(position) otherwise panic("implParamSpelling: paramDeclaredNames.get OOB at {position} — a signature's param columns are built in lockstep")
end 'implParamSpelling'

// How an impl parameter prints in E3016: the alias it was declared under where it was, else the rendered
// type. The message therefore quotes the same spelling `signatureMatches` compared as a brand.
function implParamDisplay(project Project, implFunc IrFunction, implSig FuncSignature, position ParamCount, conformingType ByteArray, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns ByteArray
	let implType = try implFunc.maxonParamTypes.get(position) otherwise panic("implParamDisplay: maxonParamTypes.get OOB at {position}")
	if maxonTypeTag(implType) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'maySpellABrand'
		let spelled = implParamSpelling(implSig, position: position)
		if not spelled.isEmpty() 'declaredThroughAnAlias'
			return spelled
		end 'declaredThroughAnAlias'
	end 'maySpellABrand'

	return renderDeclaredTypeName(implType, declarationName: conformingType, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, typeNames: project.typeNames, signatures: signatures)
end 'implParamDisplay'

function formatInterfaceMethodSignature(method InterfaceMethodSig) returns String
	return assembleMethodSignature(method.name, paramNames: method.paramNames, paramTypes: method.paramTypeNames, returnType: method.returnTypeName, isStatic: method.isStatic)
end 'formatInterfaceMethodSignature'

// Render an impl method's RETURN as a source string — `void` for a void return, else the type through the
// shared renderer. Returns a ByteArray so it feeds BOTH the string comparison in `signatureMatches` and
// the message assembly in `formatActualSignature` without a re-conversion at either.
function renderReturnType(project Project, returnType MaxonReturnType, conformingType ByteArray, typeParamNames ByteArrayArray, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns ByteArray
	return match returnType 'r'
		void gives InterfaceVoidTypeName
		value(inner) gives renderDeclaredTypeName(inner, declarationName: conformingType, typeParamNames: typeParamNames, typeNames: project.typeNames, signatures: signatures)
	end 'r'
end 'renderReturnType'

// Substitute the conforming type for an interface's `Self` — every other spelling passes through. The
// interface renders `Self` for a receiver-typed result/parameter; the conforming type's own method spells
// the concrete type, so this is what lets `returns Self` match `returns Point`.
function substituteSelf(typeName ByteArray, conformingType ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if typeName.equals(InterfaceSelfTypeName) 'isSelf'
		return conformingType
	end 'isSelf'
	return typeName
end 'substituteSelf'

// Join `  - item` lines with newlines — the bulleted detail block of an E3016 message.
function joinBulletedLines(items StringArray) returns String
	var rendered = ""
	for i in 0 upto items.count() 'each'
		if i > 0 'separator'
			rendered = "{rendered}\n"
		end 'separator'
		rendered = "{rendered}  - {try items.get(i) otherwise panic("joinBulletedLines: items.get OOB at {i}")}"
	end 'each'
	return rendered
end 'joinBulletedLines'
